 whose diameter is $18 \\:\\textrm{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3 ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\dfrac{18}{2}=9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\left(9\\right)^3 \n \t\t= 972\\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096780 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096589 :question/problem "What is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3.$\n\n![](images/99/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3 ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3\\:\\textrm{in}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-65c6d3b5-044f-5981-b57b-636418a9ab94" :question/id #uuid "65c6d3b5-044f-5981-b57b-636418a9ab94" :question/math-academy-id "q-13256" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3274e21594169662d7b96d0fcadbab1b78a3d944445cc84aad4d37b395bed6.png)\n\nDetermine the volume of the sphere above." :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=1\\,\\text{in}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (1)^{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a96f431-bd90-5911-bb0a-f6f6