g the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {4}^{3}=\\frac{256}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad2fc1cc-a316-5e6d-8bc2-452c3e6b5a80"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad2fc1cc-a316-5e6d-8bc2-452c3e6b5a80" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2fc1cc-a316-5e6d-8bc2-452c3e6b5a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-74df0648-bf4f-572c-a54d-d239fb520436" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95d19992-6d65-5252-a3b7-2b2a7b7164cf" "answer-74df0648-bf4f-572c-a54d-d239fb520436" "answer-98861c06-a026-5c97-9b2c-712f91cbc5d3" "answer-ff43e770-c0bf-5e18-a186-591a4d969f91" "answer-11acb6a1-23a6-52fc-a710-b432292606ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95d19992-6d65-5252-a3b7-2b2a7b7164cf" :answer/id #uuid "95d19992-6d65-5252-a3b7-2b2a7b7164cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74df0648-bf4f-572c-a54d-d239fb520436" :answer/id #uuid "74df0648-bf4f-572c-a54d-d239fb520436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98861c06-a026-5c97-9b2c-712f91cbc5d3" :answer/id #uuid "98861c06-a026-5c97-9b2c-712f91cbc5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff43e770-c0bf-5e18-a186-591a4d969f91" :answer/id #uuid "ff43e770-c0bf-5e18-a186-591a4d969f91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11acb6a1-23a6-52fc-a710-b432292606ba" :answer/id #uuid "11acb6a1-23a6-52fc-a710-b432292606ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097223 :question/problem "![](images/26/26b1e5acba63b8ee11d35e12bf0c8fd6944b7a466c9eab258ef36f6429e8ec0a.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $320\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=320\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{240}=2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186095267 :answer-field/correct "answer-224b1407-ecac-5a6e-8e6c-adc74ec895fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-224b1407-ecac-5a6e-8e6c-adc74ec895fb" "answer-bdf28cad-47b4-595a-bba1-73f9cc995bc3" "answer-a5260750-afc3-5614-a202-f4dbb1e3492b" "answer-9ddc1bd6-6f67-583c-98c6-1b94a6b8f512" "answer-5b50176b-6f7f-52c8-9174-4ff699e6344a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-224b1407-ecac-5a6e-8e6c-adc74ec895fb" :answer/id #uuid "224b1407-ecac-5a6e-8e6c-adc74ec895fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf28cad-47b4-595a-bba1-73f9cc995bc3" :answer/id #uuid "bdf28cad-47b4-595a-bba1-73f9cc995bc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{30}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5260750-afc3-5614-a202-f4dbb1e3492b" :answer/id #uuid "a5260750-afc3-5614-a202-f4dbb1e3492b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{20}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ddc1bd6-6f67-583c-98c6-1b94a6b8f512" :answer/id #uuid "9ddc1bd6-6f67-583c-98c6-1b94a6b8f512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b50176b-6f7f-52c8-9174-4ff699e6344a" :answer/id #uuid "5b50176b-6f7f-52c8-9174-4ff699e6344a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{30}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" :question/id #uuid "8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-33799" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf8bd7c7679e1c1df6c336b48892964c7658ac877b5efc22b099b1e98129d9ac.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343}{6}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{343}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d" :answer-field/id #uuid "20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer-field/choices ["answer-760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" "answer-17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" "answer-32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" "answer-c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" "answer-ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" :answer/id #uuid "760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" :answer/id #uuid "17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" :answer/id #uuid "32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf" :answer/id #uuid "ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097886 :tutorial/content "Given a sphere with radius $r= 3\\:\\textrm{cm},$ how do we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/8d/8df0347cb30ac58b9cbcef4b7a7e25dfd35c899b1c833cdf2e8e22bcfc51971f.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV= \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=3\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\cdot (3)^3\n=36 \\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation. In this case, we get\n\n$$\nV \\approx 113.10 \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096914 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186189904 :answer-field/choices 17592186189905] [:db/retract 17592186189904 :answer-field/choices 17592186189906] [:db/retract 17592186189904 :answer-field/choices 17592186189907] [:db/retract 17592186189904 :answer-field/choices 17592186189908] [:db/retract 17592186189904 :answer-field/choices 17592186189909] {:db/id 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-16fdfda4-c2d1-55b9-ac4e-fb5a2493ec8f" "question-56a829fb-a840-50d6-85ba-f7cb746a9aa5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" "question-374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03af7723-aeb4-55f1-8ed6-486c6b158e90" "question-a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-16fdfda4-c2d1-55b9-ac4e-fb5a2493ec8f" :question/id #uuid "16fdfda4-c2d1-55b9-ac4e-fb5a2493ec8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-148735" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =-\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(-\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93c160c4-3699-526a-aa75-85488137bfed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93c160c4-3699-526a-aa75-85488137bfed" :answer-field/id #uuid "93c160c4-3699-526a-aa75-85488137bfed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" "answer-d8c6a5b9-c9bf-5d05-847f-e7d34bd69641" "answer-2395b540-2627-5ab1-83d5-405723b43cb3" "answer-4bd61b85-6adb-58d6-bd60-2cc7be863377" "answer-755b6adb-f545-5977-ab1a-a3249a50e72f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" :answer/id #uuid "fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8c6a5b9-c9bf-5d05-847f-e7d34bd69641" :answer/id #uuid "d8c6a5b9-c9bf-5d05-847f-e7d34bd69641" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27(x+2)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2395b540-2627-5ab1-83d5-405723b43cb3" :answer/id #uuid "2395b540-2627-5ab1-83d5-405723b43cb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27-8{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bd61b85-6adb-58d6-bd60-2cc7be863377" :answer/id #uuid "4bd61b85-6adb-58d6-bd60-2cc7be863377" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-755b6adb-f545-5977-ab1a-a3249a50e72f" :answer/id #uuid "755b6adb-f545-5977-ab1a-a3249a50e72f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56a829fb-a840-50d6-85ba-f7cb746a9aa5" :question/id #uuid "56a829fb-a840-50d6-85ba-f7cb746a9aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-148734" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =2(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(2(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =8(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a43f1807-b8f6-5338-8144-d4033ce49512"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a43f1807-b8f6-5338-8144-d4033ce49512" :answer-field/id #uuid "a43f1807-b8f6-5338-8144-d4033ce49512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dec3e383-be88-58cc-a27f-e0a2319a7eb6" "answer-fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" "answer-9db8fff9-fc74-5384-890d-71e40b070419" "answer-8ccc9616-d536-5517-8214-1ef54b80d911" "answer-980ba604-0d0c-5c12-b156-6bec05449b25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec3e383-be88-58cc-a27f-e0a2319a7eb6" :answer/id #uuid "dec3e383-be88-58cc-a27f-e0a2319a7eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x+3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" :answer/id #uuid "fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9db8fff9-fc74-5384-890d-71e40b070419" :answer/id #uuid "9db8fff9-fc74-5384-890d-71e40b070419" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8{x}^{3}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ccc9616-d536-5517-8214-1ef54b80d911" :answer/id #uuid "8ccc9616-d536-5517-8214-1ef54b80d911" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(x-3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-980ba604-0d0c-5c12-b156-6bec05449b25" :answer/id #uuid "980ba604-0d0c-5c12-b156-6bec05449b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" :question/id #uuid "672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-270765" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc" :answer-field/id #uuid "75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" :question/id #uuid "374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148745" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ x-5 & =-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<5,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d" :answer-field/id #uuid "23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" "answer-4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" "answer-e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" "answer-2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" "answer-63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" :answer/id #uuid "8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{8(x-5)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" :answer/id #uuid "4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{9}(x-5)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" :answer/id #uuid "e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8}{3(x-5)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1" :answer/id #uuid "63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64}(x-5)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" :question/id #uuid "300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148557" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-3} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y-3})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{3}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" "answer-fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" "answer-bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" "answer-47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" "answer-db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" :answer/id #uuid "df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{3}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" :answer/id #uuid "fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{3}}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" :answer/id #uuid "bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{4{x}^{3}}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" :answer/id #uuid "47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193003 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{2x+3}-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{2x+3}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+3} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y+3})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y+3 & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y & =(x+4)^{3}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189904 :answer-field/correct "answer-c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" :answer-field/choices ["answer-791d0095-f94d-537e-8cbc-ca34d90cec5b" "answer-646768b3-6e7f-503c-ba19-015971768b90" "answer-c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" "answer-05b5ccdc-248f-5494-be14-486777a3d0ca" "answer-0ca8e067-88c2-59dc-9212-7b349198dbc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-791d0095-f94d-537e-8cbc-ca34d90cec5b" :answer/id #uuid "791d0095-f94d-537e-8cbc-ca34d90cec5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(2x+4)^{3}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-646768b3-6e7f-503c-ba19-015971768b90" :answer/id #uuid "646768b3-6e7f-503c-ba19-015971768b90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(2x-4)^{3}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" :answer/id #uuid "c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05b5ccdc-248f-5494-be14-486777a3d0ca" :answer/id #uuid "05b5ccdc-248f-5494-be14-486777a3d0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)^{3}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ca8e067-88c2-59dc-9212-7b349198dbc8" :answer/id #uuid "0ca8e067-88c2-59dc-9212-7b349198dbc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03af7723-aeb4-55f1-8ed6-486c6b158e90" :question/id #uuid "03af7723-aeb4-55f1-8ed6-486c6b158e90" :question/math-academy-id "q-277843" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=32(2-x)^{3}-2.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =k(2-x) \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y+4})}^{3} & ={(k(2-x))}^{3} \\\\ 2y+4 & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3} \\\\ 2y & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3}-4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}=32\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b029e56d-3e32-5c49-bf49-62786ef1d0e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b029e56d-3e32-5c49-bf49-62786ef1d0e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b029e56d-3e32-5c49-bf49-62786ef1d0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787" :answer/id #uuid "7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" :question/id #uuid "a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-148573" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5,\\,x>-k\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-2,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{2}{3(x-5)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\nk=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707" :answer-field/id #uuid "02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" "answer-e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" "answer-a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" "answer-0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" "answer-d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" :answer/id #uuid "62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" :answer/id #uuid "e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" :answer/id #uuid "a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5" :answer/id #uuid "d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110725 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" :question/id #uuid "c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106134" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cd1c9ab91a23fae12dd45078ec44ca55e69da8aa787775d7a9583857a84cb71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{11}{6}$ and $\\frac{25}{6},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola." :question/worked-solution "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{25}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{25}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{36}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15067fae-69bc-5d5f-83e8-e1ebdc618e5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15067fae-69bc-5d5f-83e8-e1ebdc618e5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "15067fae-69bc-5d5f-83e8-e1ebdc618e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f412a256-5361-5b16-a302-db1d43b422fc" "answer-4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" "answer-2f22d275-c75b-5b64-9666-30863a46ed54" "answer-76e3d3fa-3854-5450-9938-38191ec8e7d5" "answer-0824024b-c099-527a-8256-d5a6127f2786"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f412a256-5361-5b16-a302-db1d43b422fc" :answer/id #uuid "f412a256-5361-5b16-a302-db1d43b422fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/id #uuid "4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f22d275-c75b-5b64-9666-30863a46ed54" :answer/id #uuid "2f22d275-c75b-5b64-9666-30863a46ed54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76e3d3fa-3854-5450-9938-38191ec8e7d5" :answer/id #uuid "76e3d3fa-3854-5450-9938-38191ec8e7d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{35}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0824024b-c099-527a-8256-d5a6127f2786" :answer/id #uuid "0824024b-c099-527a-8256-d5a6127f2786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110726 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f8a844e-8d7a-52ec-932d-abb0df6525f2" "question-3c9a4b7b-8e3b-5aa3-8825-873abec27dfc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f8a844e-8d7a-52ec-932d-abb0df6525f2" :question/id #uuid "4f8a844e-8d7a-52ec-932d-abb0df6525f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106157" :question/problem "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=-(x-5)(x+7).$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=-(x-5)(x+7),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-7.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+(-7)}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-633578f8-1fcc-5369-8160-ab9262e6ed6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-633578f8-1fcc-5369-8160-ab9262e6ed6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "633578f8-1fcc-5369-8160-ab9262e6ed6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebb0e4ca-74ad-5d0c-b490-4cbfbd48e530" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebb0e4ca-74ad-5d0c-b490-4cbfbd48e530" "answer-dccfbbc1-9cf7-51b9-ac74-1a8d552f82e5" "answer-4ca5d762-c81a-57fc-9843-502e7d1af843" "answer-74a90c6d-2efa-51c6-a91a-aaf6755d1abb" "answer-b9780710-3705-5098-9242-2c8b7fe1151f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb0e4ca-74ad-5d0c-b490-4cbfbd48e530" :answer/id #uuid "ebb0e4ca-74ad-5d0c-b490-4cbfbd48e530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dccfbbc1-9cf7-51b9-ac74-1a8d552f82e5" :answer/id #uuid "dccfbbc1-9cf7-51b9-ac74-1a8d552f82e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca5d762-c81a-57fc-9843-502e7d1af843" :answer/id #uuid "4ca5d762-c81a-57fc-9843-502e7d1af843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74a90c6d-2efa-51c6-a91a-aaf6755d1abb" :answer/id #uuid "74a90c6d-2efa-51c6-a91a-aaf6755d1abb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9780710-3705-5098-9242-2c8b7fe1151f" :answer/id #uuid "b9780710-3705-5098-9242-2c8b7fe1151f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c9a4b7b-8e3b-5aa3-8825-873abec27dfc" :question/id #uuid "3c9a4b7b-8e3b-5aa3-8825-873abec27dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-223666" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x-1)(x-9)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=(x-1)(x-9),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=9.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff2d3b83-418c-57aa-abbd-ad23104f3a79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff2d3b83-418c-57aa-abbd-ad23104f3a79" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2d3b83-418c-57aa-abbd-ad23104f3a79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7fb5a3b2-6779-5f18-aee9-35c71d01843c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fb5a3b2-6779-5f18-aee9-35c71d01843c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fb5a3b2-6779-5f18-aee9-35c71d01843c" :answer/id #uuid "7fb5a3b2-6779-5f18-aee9-35c71d01843c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-922fea49-6b8a-58c7-b90e-f0eeab5445ca" :activity/id #uuid "922fea49-6b8a-58c7-b90e-f0eeab5445ca" :activity/title "The Average of the Roots Formula" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050577 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-fc0ea0dd-6d8e-54af-8a2c-d3dfe94d4d67" "step-7d16b69a-5a84-5d56-969f-c9e96234e18c" "step-cd4f8847-652c-5cee-968e-bc3d8b8c3f08"] :activity/first-step "step-fc0ea0dd-6d8e-54af-8a2c-d3dfe94d4d67" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-fc0ea0dd-6d8e-54af-8a2c-d3dfe94d4d67" :step/id #uuid "fc0ea0dd-6d8e-54af-8a2c-d3dfe94d4d67" :step/content "question-c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" :step/next "step-7d16b69a-5a84-5d56-969f-c9e96234e18c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7d16b69a-5a84-5d56-969f-c9e96234e18c" :step/id #uuid "7d16b69a-5a84-5d56-969f-c9e96234e18c" :step/content "question-4f8a844e-8d7a-52ec-932d-abb0df6525f2" :step/next "step-cd4f8847-652c-5cee-968e-bc3d8b8c3f08" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd4f8847-652c-5cee-968e-bc3d8b8c3f08" :step/id #uuid "cd4f8847-652c-5cee-968e-bc3d8b8c3f08" :step/content "question-3c9a4b7b-8e3b-5aa3-8825-873abec27dfc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da9b8a15-279d-5af5-bedd-335abf7ae54a" "question-9a623800-a9e7-562e-b4c1-2e4906af2084"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7135217a-2b68-531f-be98-4ee4582b3546" "question-f4424f4e-4e2c-59a3-8d15-5c180b952f0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-da9b8a15-279d-5af5-bedd-335abf7ae54a" :question/id #uuid "da9b8a15-279d-5af5-bedd-335abf7ae54a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107132" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. $3t-2{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3t-2{t}^{3}=3\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $1+t$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set\n$\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2283e84f-19db-5b85-af87-63a9f95b5c64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2283e84f-19db-5b85-af87-63a9f95b5c64" :answer-field/id #uuid "2283e84f-19db-5b85-af87-63a9f95b5c64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b8c1ea6-6462-530b-96af-e639af83f207" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b8c1ea6-6462-530b-96af-e639af83f207" "answer-1d84e348-49ea-5141-a3ac-8bf716deab2d" "answer-4e4ff0b0-3679-51c5-bd20-387e6523d247" "answer-4c2fbff4-5712-5066-8565-3259d202df8e" "answer-14c3fc10-a6da-5b0b-aa7d-90a9bad9247b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b8c1ea6-6462-530b-96af-e639af83f207" :answer/id #uuid "5b8c1ea6-6462-530b-96af-e639af83f207" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d84e348-49ea-5141-a3ac-8bf716deab2d" :answer/id #uuid "1d84e348-49ea-5141-a3ac-8bf716deab2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e4ff0b0-3679-51c5-bd20-387e6523d247" :answer/id #uuid "4e4ff0b0-3679-51c5-bd20-387e6523d247" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c2fbff4-5712-5066-8565-3259d202df8e" :answer/id #uuid "4c2fbff4-5712-5066-8565-3259d202df8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c3fc10-a6da-5b0b-aa7d-90a9bad9247b" :answer/id #uuid "14c3fc10-a6da-5b0b-aa7d-90a9bad9247b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124966 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. ${t}^{2}-2t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2t=0\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot t+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122396 :answer-field/correct 17592186122399 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a623800-a9e7-562e-b4c1-2e4906af2084" :question/id #uuid "9a623800-a9e7-562e-b4c1-2e4906af2084" :question/math-academy-id "q-107127" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2t+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (t+1)=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere all three coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2t+1=0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (t+1).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65924cba-b05a-57b8-addb-a6b67c843b8c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65924cba-b05a-57b8-addb-a6b67c843b8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "65924cba-b05a-57b8-addb-a6b67c843b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66e98276-9713-51b2-b083-bc615faf49da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2f35f63-cba0-536a-afe1-17f5848ef3a9" "answer-e8f83954-18fe-5299-b633-e8c83081d25b" "answer-f5cac527-e61f-5f2f-85b0-59626d9e66db" "answer-ba6fbd53-974d-5f48-98f5-31381cab21d6" "answer-66e98276-9713-51b2-b083-bc615faf49da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2f35f63-cba0-536a-afe1-17f5848ef3a9" :answer/id #uuid "b2f35f63-cba0-536a-afe1-17f5848ef3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8f83954-18fe-5299-b633-e8c83081d25b" :answer/id #uuid "e8f83954-18fe-5299-b633-e8c83081d25b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5cac527-e61f-5f2f-85b0-59626d9e66db" :answer/id #uuid "f5cac527-e61f-5f2f-85b0-59626d9e66db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba6fbd53-974d-5f48-98f5-31381cab21d6" :answer/id #uuid "ba6fbd53-974d-5f48-98f5-31381cab21d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66e98276-9713-51b2-b083-bc615faf49da" :answer/id #uuid "66e98276-9713-51b2-b083-bc615faf49da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" "question-382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125002 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122612 :answer-field/correct 17592186122616 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" :question/id #uuid "ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-107535" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02" :answer-field/id #uuid "62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" "answer-79fc7d50-2157-579c-9285-9ef0a16c6060" "answer-ab7e68b0-ecab-5c63-a1f5-236ebb590064" "answer-064be7dc-c00e-551d-b59c-9b5c0cc3eb52" "answer-e9f4bed5-b3f8-50de-9ea2-abc0ad3054b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" :answer/id #uuid "bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fc7d50-2157-579c-9285-9ef0a16c6060" :answer/id #uuid "79fc7d50-2157-579c-9285-9ef0a16c6060" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab7e68b0-ecab-5c63-a1f5-236ebb590064" :answer/id #uuid "ab7e68b0-ecab-5c63-a1f5-236ebb590064" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-064be7dc-c00e-551d-b59c-9b5c0cc3eb52" :answer/id #uuid "064be7dc-c00e-551d-b59c-9b5c0cc3eb52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f4bed5-b3f8-50de-9ea2-abc0ad3054b1" :answer/id #uuid "e9f4bed5-b3f8-50de-9ea2-abc0ad3054b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" :question/id #uuid "382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94869" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" :answer-field/choices ["answer-259c4561-f4f6-5e22-b3d1-59bfa17cceb6" "answer-28648a1d-d4da-54ce-9c6d-02600da5d23c" "answer-93ebf0c4-eb4c-55d4-8425-5fd308be1a58" "answer-b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" "answer-8a0ab33a-2f17-5383-81d5-4116be86b3ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-259c4561-f4f6-5e22-b3d1-59bfa17cceb6" :answer/id #uuid "259c4561-f4f6-5e22-b3d1-59bfa17cceb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28648a1d-d4da-54ce-9c6d-02600da5d23c" :answer/id #uuid "28648a1d-d4da-54ce-9c6d-02600da5d23c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93ebf0c4-eb4c-55d4-8425-5fd308be1a58" :answer/id #uuid "93ebf0c4-eb4c-55d4-8425-5fd308be1a58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" :answer/id #uuid "b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a0ab33a-2f17-5383-81d5-4116be86b3ab" :answer/id #uuid "8a0ab33a-2f17-5383-81d5-4116be86b3ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7135217a-2b68-531f-be98-4ee4582b3546" :question/id #uuid "7135217a-2b68-531f-be98-4ee4582b3546" :question/math-academy-id "q-36478" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{3}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$, the given set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false since the vector ${b}_{3}$ can't be written as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}.$ But then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9474a4f7-d9d7-5966-bcd5-2d786ecd797e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9474a4f7-d9d7-5966-bcd5-2d786ecd797e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9474a4f7-d9d7-5966-bcd5-2d786ecd797e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e7a4fd87-0d62-525b-8e48-73d80e64732e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c10d3e1f-2274-5367-bc4b-0e95df0e5a77" "answer-b2e23238-bfec-5704-ba16-5d10f1e57bb4" "answer-e7a4fd87-0d62-525b-8e48-73d80e64732e" "answer-e045873a-b588-5fec-8b66-2507fea13467" "answer-86341e77-1f57-5302-bb6d-a2c8a5537faa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c10d3e1f-2274-5367-bc4b-0e95df0e5a77" :answer/id #uuid "c10d3e1f-2274-5367-bc4b-0e95df0e5a77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2e23238-bfec-5704-ba16-5d10f1e57bb4" :answer/id #uuid "b2e23238-bfec-5704-ba16-5d10f1e57bb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7a4fd87-0d62-525b-8e48-73d80e64732e" :answer/id #uuid "e7a4fd87-0d62-525b-8e48-73d80e64732e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e045873a-b588-5fec-8b66-2507fea13467" :answer/id #uuid "e045873a-b588-5fec-8b66-2507fea13467" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86341e77-1f57-5302-bb6d-a2c8a5537faa" :answer/id #uuid "86341e77-1f57-5302-bb6d-a2c8a5537faa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124967 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122402 :answer-field/correct 17592186122404 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4424f4e-4e2c-59a3-8d15-5c180b952f0f" :question/id #uuid "f4424f4e-4e2c-59a3-8d15-5c180b952f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107938" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent. Hence, the given set is not a basis for $V.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-605de236-0714-5f9b-b01a-6122dd1e3831"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-605de236-0714-5f9b-b01a-6122dd1e3831" :answer-field/id #uuid "605de236-0714-5f9b-b01a-6122dd1e3831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ceb10ac-2029-5299-9b2b-f37c6c0e52df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8f694a6-37e0-5cb0-ad91-f0bb2037328f" "answer-5ceb10ac-2029-5299-9b2b-f37c6c0e52df" "answer-524ac2a6-5a9a-5148-8d1d-5ca5d309098c" "answer-f2e1dee4-d755-5dce-9b9a-d2e2a492f1fe" "answer-ef0366e0-ac17-5dc4-9ef7-d8e912021ed4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8f694a6-37e0-5cb0-ad91-f0bb2037328f" :answer/id #uuid "f8f694a6-37e0-5cb0-ad91-f0bb2037328f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ceb10ac-2029-5299-9b2b-f37c6c0e52df" :answer/id #uuid "5ceb10ac-2029-5299-9b2b-f37c6c0e52df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-524ac2a6-5a9a-5148-8d1d-5ca5d309098c" :answer/id #uuid "524ac2a6-5a9a-5148-8d1d-5ca5d309098c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2e1dee4-d755-5dce-9b9a-d2e2a492f1fe" :answer/id #uuid "f2e1dee4-d755-5dce-9b9a-d2e2a492f1fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef0366e0-ac17-5dc4-9ef7-d8e912021ed4" :answer/id #uuid "ef0366e0-ac17-5dc4-9ef7-d8e912021ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186126596 :answer-field/choices 17592186126597] [:db/retract 17592186126596 :answer-field/choices 17592186126598] [:db/retract 17592186126596 :answer-field/choices 17592186126599] [:db/retract 17592186126596 :answer-field/choices 17592186126600] [:db/retract 17592186126596 :answer-field/choices 17592186126601] [:db/retract 17592186126602 :answer-field/choices 17592186126603] [:db/retract 17592186126602 :answer-field/choices 17592186126604] [:db/retract 17592186126602 :answer-field/choices 17592186126605] [:db/retract 17592186126602 :answer-field/choices 17592186126606] [:db/retract 17592186126602 :answer-field/choices 17592186126607] [:db/retract 17592186128083 :answer-field/choices 17592186128084] [:db/retract 17592186128083 :answer-field/choices 17592186128085] [:db/retract 17592186128083 :answer-field/choices 17592186128086] [:db/retract 17592186128083 :answer-field/choices 17592186128087] [:db/retract 17592186128083 :answer-field/choices 17592186128088] {:db/id 17592186129267 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" "question-6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" "question-9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" "question-5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" :question/id #uuid "20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-161603" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5d/5d8a94cd0df8095c78aec955c3d0f062e38410326399104644b24e981eb1db9a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00cb030e-7557-507e-9d73-d9dfccad2a51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00cb030e-7557-507e-9d73-d9dfccad2a51" :answer-field/id #uuid "00cb030e-7557-507e-9d73-d9dfccad2a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4d014df-66b9-5abe-ae05-fa223574e036" "answer-3f831b9d-82b6-5349-872b-718dde1c3078" "answer-3768d3f5-e479-570a-8f87-054f14151075" "answer-e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" "answer-d1eb0318-10db-5f50-b75e-ea3adc67bc95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4d014df-66b9-5abe-ae05-fa223574e036" :answer/id #uuid "f4d014df-66b9-5abe-ae05-fa223574e036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-r}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f831b9d-82b6-5349-872b-718dde1c3078" :answer/id #uuid "3f831b9d-82b6-5349-872b-718dde1c3078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3768d3f5-e479-570a-8f87-054f14151075" :answer/id #uuid "3768d3f5-e479-570a-8f87-054f14151075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-r}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" :answer/id #uuid "e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1eb0318-10db-5f50-b75e-ea3adc67bc95" :answer/id #uuid "d1eb0318-10db-5f50-b75e-ea3adc67bc95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129516 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$  for $x\\ge 0$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=6+y.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b194b4882dcada56a10ec69f9dae80b363ea05efaae27385ceacc9a15ba3b56f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6+y=6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126596 :answer-field/correct "answer-e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd1bd12e-1cd5-5a17-a462-56e0ee79d849" "answer-6e67f273-e80f-584c-a56c-d08f5c55b249" "answer-e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" "answer-bd707b9b-7e24-5f86-9393-637c67c6bcee" "answer-19edc691-38d0-5ad2-be38-1638ded6e729"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd1bd12e-1cd5-5a17-a462-56e0ee79d849" :answer/id #uuid "cd1bd12e-1cd5-5a17-a462-56e0ee79d849" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e67f273-e80f-584c-a56c-d08f5c55b249" :answer/id #uuid "6e67f273-e80f-584c-a56c-d08f5c55b249" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" :answer/id #uuid "e3a983f6-7f59-5aad-b3c8-497270d02f2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd707b9b-7e24-5f86-9393-637c67c6bcee" :answer/id #uuid "bd707b9b-7e24-5f86-9393-637c67c6bcee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19edc691-38d0-5ad2-be38-1638ded6e729" :answer/id #uuid "19edc691-38d0-5ad2-be38-1638ded6e729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" :question/id #uuid "6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-161609" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/14/140ac7f7d6036657a4ba8a0c6944474c2aa0dfba7f7719228cdffb4d0cf9316f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4={r}^{2}+4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3ce3bff-654b-53ac-b60d-98e14aab9c6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3ce3bff-654b-53ac-b60d-98e14aab9c6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ce3bff-654b-53ac-b60d-98e14aab9c6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6399ea9-d979-5a2c-a0ae-56134f1964db" "answer-25533cdc-be79-58c6-ab12-68206d98ec66" "answer-b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" "answer-b86b244b-9164-5eba-9967-5ba9b71c70fe" "answer-8b5ecda2-04fc-57ef-8508-76f7387c82cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6399ea9-d979-5a2c-a0ae-56134f1964db" :answer/id #uuid "d6399ea9-d979-5a2c-a0ae-56134f1964db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{r+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25533cdc-be79-58c6-ab12-68206d98ec66" :answer/id #uuid "25533cdc-be79-58c6-ab12-68206d98ec66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" :answer/id #uuid "b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b86b244b-9164-5eba-9967-5ba9b71c70fe" :answer/id #uuid "b86b244b-9164-5eba-9967-5ba9b71c70fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{r+4}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b5ecda2-04fc-57ef-8508-76f7387c82cd" :answer/id #uuid "8b5ecda2-04fc-57ef-8508-76f7387c82cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c" :question/id #uuid "9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161602" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=2$ and $z=4+y.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad4e7b48-b967-5cd8-8d67-9aafb4784107"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad4e7b48-b967-5cd8-8d67-9aafb4784107" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4e7b48-b967-5cd8-8d67-9aafb4784107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b4012ca-c40d-5331-ab80-f5f541c94add" "answer-fb84cfdc-3789-5915-a5fd-5ed0d14ca0e0" "answer-798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" "answer-3d3cd1d4-431a-5fb2-83e3-87f48ccd6d71" "answer-658b97a0-eabd-507b-8d2d-737539308798"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b4012ca-c40d-5331-ab80-f5f541c94add" :answer/id #uuid "2b4012ca-c40d-5331-ab80-f5f541c94add" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb84cfdc-3789-5915-a5fd-5ed0d14ca0e0" :answer/id #uuid "fb84cfdc-3789-5915-a5fd-5ed0d14ca0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" :answer/id #uuid "798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d3cd1d4-431a-5fb2-83e3-87f48ccd6d71" :answer/id #uuid "3d3cd1d4-431a-5fb2-83e3-87f48ccd6d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-658b97a0-eabd-507b-8d2d-737539308798" :answer/id #uuid "658b97a0-eabd-507b-8d2d-737539308798" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186367666 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/36/36839d1fbde65e86df7335d40374fe72a5ff43ed92b89405327406a62dc8763c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129517 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126602 :answer-field/correct "answer-f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-267e1e75-5ebd-55ab-842b-e40b9ba9c3d5" "answer-f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" "answer-5c988fe0-3022-55d5-a9a9-c2db39cbe076" "answer-d507184c-2f44-5f85-9623-216dcd8ab2a5" "answer-a295bb42-7a73-51b6-8777-f5e3a19c436a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-267e1e75-5ebd-55ab-842b-e40b9ba9c3d5" :answer/id #uuid "267e1e75-5ebd-55ab-842b-e40b9ba9c3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" :answer/id #uuid "f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c988fe0-3022-55d5-a9a9-c2db39cbe076" :answer/id #uuid "5c988fe0-3022-55d5-a9a9-c2db39cbe076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d507184c-2f44-5f85-9623-216dcd8ab2a5" :answer/id #uuid "d507184c-2f44-5f85-9623-216dcd8ab2a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a295bb42-7a73-51b6-8777-f5e3a19c436a" :answer/id #uuid "a295bb42-7a73-51b6-8777-f5e3a19c436a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" :question/id #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" :question/math-academy-id "q-161628" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" "answer-8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" "answer-acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" "answer-26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" "answer-24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" :answer/id #uuid "0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" :answer/id #uuid "acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" :answer/id #uuid "26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74" :answer/id #uuid "24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186367722 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3c/3c03dde21942c60de527ace896bb51ad8070781126d72f5be5e2f6069700736d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(-3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-8,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,-8\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" :question/id #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-161631" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" "answer-28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" "answer-fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" "answer-3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" "answer-f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" :answer/id #uuid "c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" :answer/id #uuid "28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" :answer/id #uuid "fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e" :answer/id #uuid "f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186367694 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{9}{2}r-\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{9}{2}(3)-\\frac{1}{6}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{27}{2}\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{9}{4}(0-0) \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186367687 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/e2/e289cfbd210112a619018a7b905f7f2b1a9859ecb84b2517dd745705624d79d7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\sqrt{3})^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =9(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129767 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$  for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=2+x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/063fafc4f8c50dba56bcfb50b8366a2ffb8c713f5668ce22d10c0370376bbaba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+x=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r[{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r((2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r+4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2{r}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2(3)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }18+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[18\\theta +36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =18(\\pi -0)+36(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128083 :answer-field/correct "answer-98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b933786-9d3d-57c2-84f4-835643209b18" "answer-5e342a92-b054-5f7f-89ee-488cce153f9d" "answer-98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" "answer-c25b0420-ae5a-5adf-91a4-f4c1d49d88ae" "answer-68b68f0f-6620-5a61-979c-32fad52a95ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b933786-9d3d-57c2-84f4-835643209b18" :answer/id #uuid "2b933786-9d3d-57c2-84f4-835643209b18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e342a92-b054-5f7f-89ee-488cce153f9d" :answer/id #uuid "5e342a92-b054-5f7f-89ee-488cce153f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" :answer/id #uuid "98a247f0-d378-52a2-8196-59d5ace42597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c25b0420-ae5a-5adf-91a4-f4c1d49d88ae" :answer/id #uuid "c25b0420-ae5a-5adf-91a4-f4c1d49d88ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68b68f0f-6620-5a61-979c-32fad52a95ff" :answer/id #uuid "68b68f0f-6620-5a61-979c-32fad52a95ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186108967 :answer-field/choices 17592186108968] [:db/retract 17592186108967 :answer-field/choices 17592186108969] [:db/retract 17592186108967 :answer-field/choices 17592186108970] [:db/retract 17592186108967 :answer-field/choices 17592186108971] [:db/retract 17592186108967 :answer-field/choices 17592186108972] {:db/id 17592186110625 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7c172a4f-97f6-5647-a1db-039307497883"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111207 :question/problem "At the beginning of a study, a beehive had $4\\,000$ bees. The study showed that the number of bees in the hive doubles every week. How many bees will be in the hive after $3$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "The hive starts with $4,000$ and then doubles every week. \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, there are $4\\,000\\cdot 2$ bees.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, there are $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{2}$ bees.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, there are $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{3}$ bees.\n\nSo the correct expression for the number of bees after $3$ weeks is $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108967 :answer-field/correct "answer-d007a8b5-2170-5d21-b10b-72e88b23f82b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-859e2913-17e3-525b-9278-80be19f14031" "answer-932a8a67-07d6-529f-b6fb-4e505a7bfc06" "answer-75587b10-3e3b-50f3-878a-02e1543f20c8" "answer-d007a8b5-2170-5d21-b10b-72e88b23f82b" "answer-a9d71347-d0ac-57df-822a-564d3f81155f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-859e2913-17e3-525b-9278-80be19f14031" :answer/id #uuid "859e2913-17e3-525b-9278-80be19f14031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\cdot 4\\,000)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-932a8a67-07d6-529f-b6fb-4e505a7bfc06" :answer/id #uuid "932a8a67-07d6-529f-b6fb-4e505a7bfc06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75587b10-3e3b-50f3-878a-02e1543f20c8" :answer/id #uuid "75587b10-3e3b-50f3-878a-02e1543f20c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cdot 4\\,{000}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d007a8b5-2170-5d21-b10b-72e88b23f82b" :answer/id #uuid "d007a8b5-2170-5d21-b10b-72e88b23f82b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9d71347-d0ac-57df-822a-564d3f81155f" :answer/id #uuid "a9d71347-d0ac-57df-822a-564d3f81155f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{4}\\cdot 4\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" "question-1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" :question/id #uuid "ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" :question/math-academy-id "q-61650" :question/problem "Rose's initial salary was $$10\\,000$, and her salary increased by $12%$ per year. To the nearest $$10$, what was Rose's salary $3$ years later?" :question/worked-solution "Rose received $$10\\,000$ initially, and this amount increased by $12%$ each year.\n\n$12%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money received after $3$ years, we get\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}≈14\\,050.\n$$\n\nAfter $3$ years, Rose received approximately $14\\,050$ dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04d9141d-29ea-5c02-a238-48a843d162a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04d9141d-29ea-5c02-a238-48a843d162a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "04d9141d-29ea-5c02-a238-48a843d162a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25bfa7fe-6bbc-5bba-8456-d285f71fb72d" "answer-4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" "answer-ce20cd5b-5a77-5b24-a7f2-4481ecd81f4c" "answer-3aec392f-ba73-525c-8b12-dfcceda07e38" "answer-bd58d7b9-4b69-5345-98e8-c2b4988aeceb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25bfa7fe-6bbc-5bba-8456-d285f71fb72d" :answer/id #uuid "25bfa7fe-6bbc-5bba-8456-d285f71fb72d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$13\\,430$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" :answer/id #uuid "4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$14\\,050$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce20cd5b-5a77-5b24-a7f2-4481ecd81f4c" :answer/id #uuid "ce20cd5b-5a77-5b24-a7f2-4481ecd81f4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$14\\,830$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aec392f-ba73-525c-8b12-dfcceda07e38" :answer/id #uuid "3aec392f-ba73-525c-8b12-dfcceda07e38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$15\\,660$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd58d7b9-4b69-5345-98e8-c2b4988aeceb" :answer/id #uuid "bd58d7b9-4b69-5345-98e8-c2b4988aeceb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$15\\,970$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c172a4f-97f6-5647-a1db-039307497883" :question/id #uuid "7c172a4f-97f6-5647-a1db-039307497883" :question/math-academy-id "q-61834" :question/problem "One month, a greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon. If the number of kilograms of sold watermelon doubles each month, how many kilograms of watermelon will the greengrocer sell after $4$ months?" :question/worked-solution "In a particular month, the greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon, and then this amount doubles every month.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ month, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot 2$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $2$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{2}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $3$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{3}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $4$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{4}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\nSo the correct expression is $40\\cdot {2}^{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c4f58b6-58c2-5e54-bc66-efc59367c283"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c4f58b6-58c2-5e54-bc66-efc59367c283" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c4f58b6-58c2-5e54-bc66-efc59367c283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4dde1711-99e2-58fb-89e6-decf4b78c670" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c387e36d-a0c7-5230-a0e8-64529a3cc938" "answer-0a3652a9-19a0-57cd-8ad1-d828e5b3ffad" "answer-3342e9e2-c937-5e04-bfee-4d4d83519f3b" "answer-4dde1711-99e2-58fb-89e6-decf4b78c670" "answer-09ac0039-4090-5467-a2a3-64bb72608f51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c387e36d-a0c7-5230-a0e8-64529a3cc938" :answer/id #uuid "c387e36d-a0c7-5230-a0e8-64529a3cc938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(40\\cdot 2)^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a3652a9-19a0-57cd-8ad1-d828e5b3ffad" :answer/id #uuid "0a3652a9-19a0-57cd-8ad1-d828e5b3ffad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(40\\cdot 4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3342e9e2-c937-5e04-bfee-4d4d83519f3b" :answer/id #uuid "3342e9e2-c937-5e04-bfee-4d4d83519f3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\cdot 2\\cdot 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dde1711-99e2-58fb-89e6-decf4b78c670" :answer/id #uuid "4dde1711-99e2-58fb-89e6-decf4b78c670" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\cdot {2}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09ac0039-4090-5467-a2a3-64bb72608f51" :answer/id #uuid "09ac0039-4090-5467-a2a3-64bb72608f51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\cdot {4}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fb8258a2-da79-5e0d-b31f-678a453a5a7e" :activity/id #uuid "fb8258a2-da79-5e0d-b31f-678a453a5a7e" :activity/title "Creating Exponential Growth Expressions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050886 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-cd3ae942-426d-56b9-afd0-f43d65368750" "step-cfda6eef-02c1-5673-89c5-c06fdbcb6a52" "step-84d2439c-12b2-5f43-8e1a-04cf836fad55"] :activity/first-step "step-cd3ae942-426d-56b9-afd0-f43d65368750" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd3ae942-426d-56b9-afd0-f43d65368750" :step/id #uuid "cd3ae942-426d-56b9-afd0-f43d65368750" :step/content 17592186111207 :step/next "step-cfda6eef-02c1-5673-89c5-c06fdbcb6a52" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cfda6eef-02c1-5673-89c5-c06fdbcb6a52" :step/id #uuid "cfda6eef-02c1-5673-89c5-c06fdbcb6a52" :step/content "question-ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" :step/next "step-84d2439c-12b2-5f43-8e1a-04cf836fad55" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-84d2439c-12b2-5f43-8e1a-04cf836fad55" :step/id #uuid "84d2439c-12b2-5f43-8e1a-04cf836fad55" :step/content "question-7c172a4f-97f6-5647-a1db-039307497883" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083188 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35646e02-dadb-58eb-b337-8ae59fb6fd33"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-35646e02-dadb-58eb-b337-8ae59fb6fd33" :question/id #uuid "35646e02-dadb-58eb-b337-8ae59fb6fd33" :question/math-academy-id "q-30392" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=k{x}^{2}+3x-15$ is $x=4.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $4.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=k$ and $b=3$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{2(k)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2k} & =4 \\\\ -3 & =8k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8376f657-f588-5dd0-82b6-5a3fe8715894"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8376f657-f588-5dd0-82b6-5a3fe8715894" :answer-field/id #uuid "8376f657-f588-5dd0-82b6-5a3fe8715894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eea8ff49-8522-55f7-a1dd-f797ae1ba599" :answer-field/choices ["answer-140d1a26-6c40-56b2-a7aa-06cc13939aeb" "answer-641005cd-8b3f-5949-aba8-d33285377340" "answer-eea8ff49-8522-55f7-a1dd-f797ae1ba599" "answer-68c8f4fe-d2ac-57e9-b77c-83c2133b2dfc" "answer-2898236d-d376-51b4-b8f7-ddd778295f25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-140d1a26-6c40-56b2-a7aa-06cc13939aeb" :answer/id #uuid "140d1a26-6c40-56b2-a7aa-06cc13939aeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-641005cd-8b3f-5949-aba8-d33285377340" :answer/id #uuid "641005cd-8b3f-5949-aba8-d33285377340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eea8ff49-8522-55f7-a1dd-f797ae1ba599" :answer/id #uuid "eea8ff49-8522-55f7-a1dd-f797ae1ba599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68c8f4fe-d2ac-57e9-b77c-83c2133b2dfc" :answer/id #uuid "68c8f4fe-d2ac-57e9-b77c-83c2133b2dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2898236d-d376-51b4-b8f7-ddd778295f25" :answer/id #uuid "2898236d-d376-51b4-b8f7-ddd778295f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083187 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa0495c1-850f-5f82-bb9f-fd1f7fa6ce97" "question-c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa0495c1-850f-5f82-bb9f-fd1f7fa6ce97" :question/id #uuid "aa0495c1-850f-5f82-bb9f-fd1f7fa6ce97" :question/math-academy-id "q-30451" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+12x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ and $b=12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{12}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}+12x+6 \\\\  & =2(-3)^{2}+12(-3)+6 \\\\  & =18-36+6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-3,-12).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6b01d69-c2df-5fe3-b016-c551c810194f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6b01d69-c2df-5fe3-b016-c551c810194f" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b01d69-c2df-5fe3-b016-c551c810194f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d97b834-effc-5e59-bf27-57494456893b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f564bb1-e463-51e8-a110-610f5d826505" "answer-9d97b834-effc-5e59-bf27-57494456893b" "answer-397d7b39-3eef-5894-9212-13f669b2cb7d" "answer-010f0520-1f81-5417-8d63-763acd75346e" "answer-f3d41fac-ad3a-578a-afb8-fec99dac9b73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f564bb1-e463-51e8-a110-610f5d826505" :answer/id #uuid "1f564bb1-e463-51e8-a110-610f5d826505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-10)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d97b834-effc-5e59-bf27-57494456893b" :answer/id #uuid "9d97b834-effc-5e59-bf27-57494456893b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-397d7b39-3eef-5894-9212-13f669b2cb7d" :answer/id #uuid "397d7b39-3eef-5894-9212-13f669b2cb7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-010f0520-1f81-5417-8d63-763acd75346e" :answer/id #uuid "010f0520-1f81-5417-8d63-763acd75346e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,38)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3d41fac-ad3a-578a-afb8-fec99dac9b73" :answer/id #uuid "f3d41fac-ad3a-578a-afb8-fec99dac9b73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,60)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" :question/id #uuid "c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-30452" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}-2x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=-2$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{-2}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9f334df-9edf-56f6-b904-ca6fc39afbf1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9f334df-9edf-56f6-b904-ca6fc39afbf1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f334df-9edf-56f6-b904-ca6fc39afbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d9e3163-77a3-5b77-b6dd-5f9cc4183e81" "answer-8720512d-ed7f-54db-9220-6ac8c52ec59f" "answer-4aaceaf8-31cb-57d9-89f4-40f6fbb0f71e" "answer-d7eb0642-fe00-5e0f-a9ab-9dcbf769b12b" "answer-f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d9e3163-77a3-5b77-b6dd-5f9cc4183e81" :answer/id #uuid "2d9e3163-77a3-5b77-b6dd-5f9cc4183e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8720512d-ed7f-54db-9220-6ac8c52ec59f" :answer/id #uuid "8720512d-ed7f-54db-9220-6ac8c52ec59f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4aaceaf8-31cb-57d9-89f4-40f6fbb0f71e" :answer/id #uuid "4aaceaf8-31cb-57d9-89f4-40f6fbb0f71e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7eb0642-fe00-5e0f-a9ab-9dcbf769b12b" :answer/id #uuid "d7eb0642-fe00-5e0f-a9ab-9dcbf769b12b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/id #uuid "f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2aedf8e5-8934-58a3-922c-e425fd7146f3" :activity/id #uuid "2aedf8e5-8934-58a3-922c-e425fd7146f3" :activity/title "The Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048398 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-ce67b857-f176-5873-ba8a-87814b9af6a7" "step-800c1a08-8dd6-5f55-92e4-df70f0fce894" "step-d9a1c56e-6c1b-5141-9c3c-939ad62ac505"] :activity/first-step "step-ce67b857-f176-5873-ba8a-87814b9af6a7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ce67b857-f176-5873-ba8a-87814b9af6a7" :step/id #uuid "ce67b857-f176-5873-ba8a-87814b9af6a7" :step/content "question-35646e02-dadb-58eb-b337-8ae59fb6fd33" :step/next "step-800c1a08-8dd6-5f55-92e4-df70f0fce894" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-800c1a08-8dd6-5f55-92e4-df70f0fce894" :step/id #uuid "800c1a08-8dd6-5f55-92e4-df70f0fce894" :step/content "question-aa0495c1-850f-5f82-bb9f-fd1f7fa6ce97" :step/next "step-d9a1c56e-6c1b-5141-9c3c-939ad62ac505" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d9a1c56e-6c1b-5141-9c3c-939ad62ac505" :step/id #uuid "d9a1c56e-6c1b-5141-9c3c-939ad62ac505" :step/content "question-c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186171303 :answer-field/choices 17592186171304] [:db/retract 17592186171303 :answer-field/choices 17592186171305] [:db/retract 17592186171303 :answer-field/choices 17592186171306] [:db/retract 17592186171303 :answer-field/choices 17592186171307] [:db/retract 17592186171303 :answer-field/choices 17592186171308] {:db/id 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172613 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171303 :answer-field/correct "answer-6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" "answer-88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" "answer-a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" "answer-c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" "answer-6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" :answer/id #uuid "87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" :answer/id #uuid "88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" :answer/id #uuid "a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" :answer/id #uuid "c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer/id #uuid "6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :question/id #uuid "f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94051" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45" :answer-field/id #uuid "6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" "answer-f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" "answer-c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" "answer-a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" "answer-fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" :answer/id #uuid "d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" :answer/id #uuid "f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" :answer/id #uuid "c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" :answer/id #uuid "a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer/id #uuid "fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8204430f-7743-5e1b-97bd-65f00f3fe35a" :activity/id #uuid "8204430f-7743-5e1b-97bd-65f00f3fe35a" :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048493 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" "step-e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" "step-546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" "step-157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" "step-589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c"] :activity/first-step "step-32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" :step/id #uuid "32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" :step/content 17592186172613 :step/next "step-e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" :step/id #uuid "e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" :step/content 17592186172615 :step/next "step-546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" :step/id #uuid "546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" :step/content 17592186357631 :step/next "step-157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" :step/id #uuid "157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" :step/content 17592186492002 :step/next "step-589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c" :step/id #uuid "589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c" :step/content "question-f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192814 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ea6de0b-579b-595d-b4da-dc4ce1abfbcc" "question-a700c45d-e998-5eef-9aa2-096588d12ebe" "question-306d067e-7a9d-57e7-9fd1-1469fa53a6b0" "question-6ad34a02-d412-58b2-a634-d74158ac441a" "question-3136287b-ab52-50e3-8963-70ea96c4eb24"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192405 :question/problem "The function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}$ is defined for $x >1.$ Find $f^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{(y-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4}{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ x\\cdot (y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{x}} \\\\ |y-1| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y > 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n|y-1| = y-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} + 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192815 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-58d6a2d3-405c-5b5e-b02f-68804a6747aa" "question-1a45b39d-afe3-5e4b-83e0-4037874d7882" "question-411f5065-9d51-51d9-906e-20eb90eb5d15" "question-500aed1e-922d-582e-a7e3-3bbfcff68302" "question-de7ae964-9fce-58b0-bd1b-ba5303123396"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192406 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{2-y}{y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2-y}{y+3} \\\\ x(y+3) & =2-y \\\\ xy+3x & =2-y \\\\ xy+y & =2-3x \\\\ y(x+1) & =2-3x \\\\ y & =\\frac{2-3x}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{x+1}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192816 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-754f4c4b-11dc-5f96-9fa9-3c28fe7e8a39" "question-e33bcefe-71b8-5e01-9202-e30bde07e783" "question-fa405f43-aa49-5df2-9659-18d1b520e384" "question-3bd71fc1-9e9b-5a64-b8c7-ee650f7e3c32" "question-36115cd9-9382-506b-9404-8a68bbaccae0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192407 :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}, \\qquad x\\neq 4\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-2}, \\qquad x\\neq 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{ky+6}{y-4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{ky+6}{y-4} \\\\ x(y-4) & =ky+6 \\\\ xy-4x & =ky+6 \\\\ xy-ky & =4x+6 \\\\ y(x-k) & =4x+6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+6}{x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-k}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ea6de0b-579b-595d-b4da-dc4ce1abfbcc" :question/id #uuid "8ea6de0b-579b-595d-b4da-dc4ce1abfbcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-276645" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>-\\frac{7}{4}.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}} \\\\ x(4y+7)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (4y+7)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y+7)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\ |4y+7| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y+7 & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>-\\frac{7}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n|4y+7|=4y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5552c58-f49c-529a-9760-0d2cd4c8e4de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5552c58-f49c-529a-9760-0d2cd4c8e4de" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5552c58-f49c-529a-9760-0d2cd4c8e4de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0cecda5c-102e-5a0a-a6ab-c0bc483ddb4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cecda5c-102e-5a0a-a6ab-c0bc483ddb4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cecda5c-102e-5a0a-a6ab-c0bc483ddb4d" :answer/id #uuid "0cecda5c-102e-5a0a-a6ab-c0bc483ddb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a700c45d-e998-5eef-9aa2-096588d12ebe" :question/id #uuid "a700c45d-e998-5eef-9aa2-096588d12ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-276634" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{6x+2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{6x+2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6y+2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{6y+2} \\\\ x(6y+2) & =5 \\\\ 6xy+2x & =5 \\\\ 6xy & =5-2x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5-2x}{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-2x}{6x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa31e955-275b-5fef-861b-ebf469837989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa31e955-275b-5fef-861b-ebf469837989" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa31e955-275b-5fef-861b-ebf469837989" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-47bbaa97-6413-54a1-9502-e9fda4b11eda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47bbaa97-6413-54a1-9502-e9fda4b11eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47bbaa97-6413-54a1-9502-e9fda4b11eda" :answer/id #uuid "47bbaa97-6413-54a1-9502-e9fda4b11eda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5-2x}{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-306d067e-7a9d-57e7-9fd1-1469fa53a6b0" :question/id #uuid "306d067e-7a9d-57e7-9fd1-1469fa53a6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-276631" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7-5x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{7-5x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{7-5y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{7-5y} \\\\ x(7-5y) & =2 \\\\ 7x-5xy & =2 \\\\ -5xy & =2-7x \\\\ 5xy & =7x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x-2}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{7x-2}{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e97ae974-ae89-5e50-835c-a14289debe35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e97ae974-ae89-5e50-835c-a14289debe35" :answer-field/id #uuid "e97ae974-ae89-5e50-835c-a14289debe35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bae21e18-9aeb-5d48-b0dd-753057a50a46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bae21e18-9aeb-5d48-b0dd-753057a50a46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bae21e18-9aeb-5d48-b0dd-753057a50a46" :answer/id #uuid "bae21e18-9aeb-5d48-b0dd-753057a50a46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7x-2}{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ad34a02-d412-58b2-a634-d74158ac441a" :question/id #uuid "6ad34a02-d412-58b2-a634-d74158ac441a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148433" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{5x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6}{5y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6}{5y+3} \\\\ x(5y+3) & =6 \\\\ 5xy+3x & =6 \\\\ 5xy & =6-3x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6-3x}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-3x}{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3de24f3-932f-5353-84ca-c93c446754ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3de24f3-932f-5353-84ca-c93c446754ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3de24f3-932f-5353-84ca-c93c446754ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-989be707-a14f-5bcd-81fd-590caf148e37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7acda287-e53d-5680-93e4-fafc30e2d30e" "answer-576bd75e-a8e9-57a8-a545-a2e5ca4d48f8" "answer-7766ddbd-6efe-5f85-9f18-88dc97242f6d" "answer-072d4511-f60e-5db1-9752-17df9083ec0e" "answer-989be707-a14f-5bcd-81fd-590caf148e37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7acda287-e53d-5680-93e4-fafc30e2d30e" :answer/id #uuid "7acda287-e53d-5680-93e4-fafc30e2d30e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x+6}{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-576bd75e-a8e9-57a8-a545-a2e5ca4d48f8" :answer/id #uuid "576bd75e-a8e9-57a8-a545-a2e5ca4d48f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x-5}{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7766ddbd-6efe-5f85-9f18-88dc97242f6d" :answer/id #uuid "7766ddbd-6efe-5f85-9f18-88dc97242f6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-3x}{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-072d4511-f60e-5db1-9752-17df9083ec0e" :answer/id #uuid "072d4511-f60e-5db1-9