mation $u(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the change-of-variable method, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{2})\\cdot |2y| \\\\  & =3({y}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3{y}^{4}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =6{y}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 1,$ the bounds are $a = 0$ and $b = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\quad u(1) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n![](images/e2/e20ebb32777cc624f0c436e59cb0be55958ab8369b345a7da890a3504b42fe34.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b66e4d9-8056-570c-838a-8b4a8b4a119c" :question/id #uuid "2b66e4d9-8056-570c-838a-8b4a8b4a119c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143767" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{{X}^{4}+1},$ where $X\\in (-2,2)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{2}+X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-2,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{2}+x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\n.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d94ef671-f7de-5c89-80ee-f193344cab7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d94ef671-f7de-5c89-80ee-f193344cab7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "d94ef671-f7de-5c89-80ee-f193344cab7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41aa6d50-25e0-5074-be28-acd0423987c1" "answer-4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" "answer-12750671-c0a4-56d1-8642-65229985be21" "answer-e5a74afd-b188-5b3c-8185-ce3e76a3ea4b" "answer-f44afbbd-61fb-51ca-818f-6b079b7bcfa3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41aa6d50-25e0-5074-be28-acd0423987c1" :answer/id #uuid "41aa6d50-25e0-5074-be28-acd0423987c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" :answer/id #uuid "4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12750671-c0a4-56d1-8642-65229985be21" :answer/id #uuid "12750671-c0a4-56d1-8642-65229985be21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5a74afd-b188-5b3c-8185-ce3e76a3ea4b" :answer/id #uuid "e5a74afd-b188-5b3c-8185-ce3e76a3ea4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f44afbbd-61fb-51ca-818f-6b079b7bcfa3" :answer/id #uuid "f44afbbd-61fb-51ca-818f-6b079b7bcfa3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68ad658e-fc68-5dd0-8313-20d02f0dbcab" :question/id #uuid "68ad658e-fc68-5dd0-8313-20d02f0dbcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-143757" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y={X}^{5}+1,$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $Y=1-{X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{5}+1$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Transformation III is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=1-{x}^{4}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2306df6b-e9b2-5ebc-be81-284b6041ab6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2306df6b-e9b2-5ebc-be81-284b6041ab6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2306df6b-e9b2-5ebc-be81-284b6041ab6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1dc62f23-318c-50d0-87d4-4f837695b5fc" "answer-c158ba71-83f0-55e5-ab39-f9b125bbc5a5" "answer-1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" "answer-0fbec0d8-f92e-5399-9366-340075456307" "answer-df26bafd-3008-5692-9ec4-c15e5e10d340"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dc62f23-318c-50d0-87d4-4f837695b5fc" :answer/id #uuid "1dc62f23-318c-50d0-87d4-4f837695b5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c158ba71-83f0-55e5-ab39-f9b125bbc5a5" :answer/id #uuid "c158ba71-83f0-55e5-ab39-f9b125bbc5a5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" :answer/id #uuid "1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fbec0d8-f92e-5399-9366-340075456307" :answer/id #uuid "0fbec0d8-f92e-5399-9366-340075456307" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df26bafd-3008-5692-9ec4-c15e5e10d340" :answer/id #uuid "df26bafd-3008-5692-9ec4-c15e5e10d340" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2be8e1ac-e4a4-5fc5-ab2e-be1784629b6e" :question/id #uuid "2be8e1ac-e4a4-5fc5-ab2e-be1784629b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143779" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=-{X}^{2},$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,1).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{4}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4afdfcbd-f7ba-5636-a6c2-9bd26c6f3ed2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4afdfcbd-f7ba-5636-a6c2-9bd26c6f3ed2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4afdfcbd-f7ba-5636-a6c2-9bd26c6f3ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe47ec8f-29bf-5b7d-8c7e-65bb3449320f" "answer-8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" "answer-2beb3def-714b-5fac-9aed-08a84b6a5e91" "answer-51140ee9-ed10-5c84-b02a-584e6f2c21e7" "answer-931f53f1-45e2-561b-b5a4-0a96990a5a23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe47ec8f-29bf-5b7d-8c7e-65bb3449320f" :answer/id #uuid "fe47ec8f-29bf-5b7d-8c7e-65bb3449320f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" :answer/id #uuid "8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2beb3def-714b-5fac-9aed-08a84b6a5e91" :answer/id #uuid "2beb3def-714b-5fac-9aed-08a84b6a5e91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51140ee9-ed10-5c84-b02a-584e6f2c21e7" :answer/id #uuid "51140ee9-ed10-5c84-b02a-584e6f2c21e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-931f53f1-45e2-561b-b5a4-0a96990a5a23" :answer/id #uuid "931f53f1-45e2-561b-b5a4-0a96990a5a23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6df473a-21d5-53f9-84c7-d9c14b1c57fc" :question/id #uuid "e6df473a-21d5-53f9-84c7-d9c14b1c57fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143756" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ where $X\\in [1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X,$ where $X\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{3},$ where $X\\in (-10,10)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{3}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-10,10).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a3f18f8-3eb5-5f43-835e-c32778388a91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a3f18f8-3eb5-5f43-835e-c32778388a91" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3f18f8-3eb5-5f43-835e-c32778388a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" "answer-f426aa49-b205-5b56-8c2b-7dfee92d75ff" "answer-522a23da-3199-5beb-840f-bbd80050fea4" "answer-d6e6decc-fb57-5713-ab4c-0492b3b343b1" "answer-e8447dd5-f351-54b0-b319-349538194370"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" :answer/id #uuid "7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f426aa49-b205-5b56-8c2b-7dfee92d75ff" :answer/id #uuid "f426aa49-b205-5b56-8c2b-7dfee92d75ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-522a23da-3199-5beb-840f-bbd80050fea4" :answer/id #uuid "522a23da-3199-5beb-840f-bbd80050fea4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6e6decc-fb57-5713-ab4c-0492b3b343b1" :answer/id #uuid "d6e6decc-fb57-5713-ab4c-0492b3b343b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8447dd5-f351-54b0-b319-349538194370" :answer/id #uuid "e8447dd5-f351-54b0-b319-349538194370" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83ef6537-86b1-5578-a678-ff969ac2e505" :question/id #uuid "83ef6537-86b1-5578-a678-ff969ac2e505" :question/math-academy-id "q-143755" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-3X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ where $X\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $Y=\\sqrt{X},$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-3x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1654b677-b5f3-51ca-bcd6-9d948b5c36fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1654b677-b5f3-51ca-bcd6-9d948b5c36fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "1654b677-b5f3-51ca-bcd6-9d948b5c36fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97d11451-774d-5db5-b500-02196e6f3a64" "answer-0cff4110-673e-5a0b-b763-dc2b18f6751a" "answer-d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" "answer-e6d292c9-efca-5a44-b466-139650cf2579" "answer-bd4db205-357c-52d8-b50a-8c12abf3a01b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97d11451-774d-5db5-b500-02196e6f3a64" :answer/id #uuid "97d11451-774d-5db5-b500-02196e6f3a64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cff4110-673e-5a0b-b763-dc2b18f6751a" :answer/id #uuid "0cff4110-673e-5a0b-b763-dc2b18f6751a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" :answer/id #uuid "d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6d292c9-efca-5a44-b466-139650cf2579" :answer/id #uuid "e6d292c9-efca-5a44-b466-139650cf2579" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4db205-357c-52d8-b50a-8c12abf3a01b" :answer/id #uuid "bd4db205-357c-52d8-b50a-8c12abf3a01b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba6a48c1-7aec-5ddc-a819-dca70319c91f" :question/id #uuid "ba6a48c1-7aec-5ddc-a819-dca70319c91f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143775" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2-X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2-X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2-x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2-y)\\cdot |-1| \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=2,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-316bf6ab-3947-532a-8211-ad09e1e9e024"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-316bf6ab-3947-532a-8211-ad09e1e9e024" :answer-field/id #uuid "316bf6ab-3947-532a-8211-ad09e1e9e024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5505216d-62d4-5def-93fc-88b1ec673ac2" "answer-94632b8d-e646-5a1c-841b-4e1b8e19abb4" "answer-d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" "answer-d0da31ad-07c0-5d9c-9e30-027174062da2" "answer-aa9cd992-4d6d-5ca5-ae3d-ef1729cd1a3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5505216d-62d4-5def-93fc-88b1ec673ac2" :answer/id #uuid "5505216d-62d4-5def-93fc-88b1ec673ac2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94632b8d-e646-5a1c-841b-4e1b8e19abb4" :answer/id #uuid "94632b8d-e646-5a1c-841b-4e1b8e19abb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(1-y)^{3}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" :answer/id #uuid "d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0da31ad-07c0-5d9c-9e30-027174062da2" :answer/id #uuid "d0da31ad-07c0-5d9c-9e30-027174062da2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa9cd992-4d6d-5ca5-ae3d-ef1729cd1a3c" :answer/id #uuid "aa9cd992-4d6d-5ca5-ae3d-ef1729cd1a3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2-y)^{3}}{15}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" :question/id #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-143774" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" "answer-1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" "answer-0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" "answer-10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" "answer-f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" :answer/id #uuid "df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" :answer/id #uuid "1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" :answer/id #uuid "10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20" :answer/id #uuid "f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cdff0a1-3127-5db2-a31d-94794e2a46b7" :question/id #uuid "4cdff0a1-3127-5db2-a31d-94794e2a46b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143777" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\, & 1\\le x\\le e \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{X}{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{u}^{-1}(y)}{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,u(e)=\\frac{e}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7865a740-1a99-5f05-a821-db2d459c2cba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7865a740-1a99-5f05-a821-db2d459c2cba" :answer-field/id #uuid "7865a740-1a99-5f05-a821-db2d459c2cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4d09561-afb0-5c88-aa32-fb6a6e01b8da" "answer-4a8f09eb-0cd1-51e6-8310-03cf1c596658" "answer-172b375e-317c-5b1c-9b41-2c052f27e8cc" "answer-d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" "answer-5da7260c-a3fd-55ce-8eb2-dbff9b05485e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4d09561-afb0-5c88-aa32-fb6a6e01b8da" :answer/id #uuid "c4d09561-afb0-5c88-aa32-fb6a6e01b8da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2y}, & 1\\le y\\le \\sqrt{e} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a8f09eb-0cd1-51e6-8310-03cf1c596658" :answer/id #uuid "4a8f09eb-0cd1-51e6-8310-03cf1c596658" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-172b375e-317c-5b1c-9b41-2c052f27e8cc" :answer/id #uuid "172b375e-317c-5b1c-9b41-2c052f27e8cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" :answer/id #uuid "d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5da7260c-a3fd-55ce-8eb2-dbff9b05485e" :answer/id #uuid "5da7260c-a3fd-55ce-8eb2-dbff9b05485e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y}, & 1\\le y\\le \\sqrt{e} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d414a04e-d0c3-5f7e-b95a-0d762c3601e3" :question/id #uuid "d414a04e-d0c3-5f7e-b95a-0d762c3601e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143764" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}y-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1}(y)-2 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2y+4)\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2y+4)^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $2<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=2$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(2)=-1,\\,u(4)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0feccc34-587e-5131-85c6-09d92716448a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0feccc34-587e-5131-85c6-09d92716448a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0feccc34-587e-5131-85c6-09d92716448a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" :answer-field/choices ["answer-936d9658-283d-560e-bc6d-ea190c5bec8c" "answer-ad9ede15-0d30-5234-a411-d8e0859ef10f" "answer-d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" "answer-bad0b915-0433-5599-97e8-4708519f4503" "answer-4010751e-b00b-5952-82c6-80435f82ec17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-936d9658-283d-560e-bc6d-ea190c5bec8c" :answer/id #uuid "936d9658-283d-560e-bc6d-ea190c5bec8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{(y+4)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad9ede15-0d30-5234-a411-d8e0859ef10f" :answer/id #uuid "ad9ede15-0d30-5234-a411-d8e0859ef10f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" :answer/id #uuid "d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bad0b915-0433-5599-97e8-4708519f4503" :answer/id #uuid "bad0b915-0433-5599-97e8-4708519f4503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+4)^{2}}, & -1<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4010751e-b00b-5952-82c6-80435f82ec17" :answer/id #uuid "4010751e-b00b-5952-82c6-80435f82ec17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6f869b2-aa11-5377-8e76-cb36213ba7a6" :question/id #uuid "c6f869b2-aa11-5377-8e76-cb36213ba7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143773" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\, & 0<x<\\pi  \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=3X+3\\pi ,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=3X+3\\pi .$ The transformation $u(x)=3x+3\\pi $ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y+3\\pi  \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y)+3\\pi  \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<\\pi ,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=3\\pi ,\\,u(\\pi )=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b92ab0a5-3040-53cf-82b8-bf078d4d8e28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b92ab0a5-3040-53cf-82b8-bf078d4d8e28" :answer-field/id #uuid "b92ab0a5-3040-53cf-82b8-bf078d4d8e28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef12e410-2dd2-54b0-8f94-d0d3d23c11aa" "answer-5a4b3c40-e6b8-525a-82c8-7ceba383c32e" "answer-f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" "answer-fe44390e-3a59-58fc-9d2b-ca5ec6e5fa5b" "answer-45a16446-72e7-5554-b9f3-37b2398be8d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef12e410-2dd2-54b0-8f94-d0d3d23c11aa" :answer/id #uuid "ef12e410-2dd2-54b0-8f94-d0d3d23c11aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a4b3c40-e6b8-525a-82c8-7ceba383c32e" :answer/id #uuid "5a4b3c40-e6b8-525a-82c8-7ceba383c32e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-3\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<4\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" :answer/id #uuid "f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe44390e-3a59-58fc-9d2b-ca5ec6e5fa5b" :answer/id #uuid "fe44390e-3a59-58fc-9d2b-ca5ec6e5fa5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a16446-72e7-5554-b9f3-37b2398be8d4" :answer/id #uuid "45a16446-72e7-5554-b9f3-37b2398be8d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-3\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<4\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff19e38b-ccbe-5836-a668-3a89307b9227" :question/id #uuid "ff19e38b-ccbe-5836-a668-3a89307b9227" :question/math-academy-id "q-143894" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{3})\\cdot |3{y}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{({y}^{3})}^{3}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{9}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(2)=\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1cfdb681-1420-5e1f-89b1-59fd61ccc9df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cfdb681-1420-5e1f-89b1-59fd61ccc9df" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfdb681-1420-5e1f-89b1-59fd61ccc9df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0334f0c-f3c9-5d23-a132-9151568d6904" "answer-2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" "answer-d7241d2b-cc21-532e-9835-5918240ff0d6" "answer-4410421e-5953-53d0-9979-891add3b5dce" "answer-4844bff0-8c04-50b9-8d99-cfda0d95e6de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0334f0c-f3c9-5d23-a132-9151568d6904" :answer/id #uuid "c0334f0c-f3c9-5d23-a132-9151568d6904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" :answer/id #uuid "2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7241d2b-cc21-532e-9835-5918240ff0d6" :answer/id #uuid "d7241d2b-cc21-532e-9835-5918240ff0d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{2}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4410421e-5953-53d0-9979-891add3b5dce" :answer/id #uuid "4410421e-5953-53d0-9979-891add3b5dce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{9}}{512}, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4844bff0-8c04-50b9-8d99-cfda0d95e6de" :answer/id #uuid "4844bff0-8c04-50b9-8d99-cfda0d95e6de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2}, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ba326fb-1af3-5ddd-8698-9863f73e0845" :question/id #uuid "2ba326fb-1af3-5ddd-8698-9863f73e0845" :question/math-academy-id "q-143895" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={X}^{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)={x}^{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ y & =({u}^{-1}(y))^{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =(2-2\\sqrt{y})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81f86259-5958-5bf3-bfcc-15c37733af30"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81f86259-5958-5bf3-bfcc-15c37733af30" :answer-field/id #uuid "81f86259-5958-5bf3-bfcc-15c37733af30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f11f15bf-525a-5a41-bdc7-38a742a7f4d9" "answer-3eb89bec-79dd-58af-8196-d3d356437ba3" "answer-19341623-c069-57f0-918f-2be57be5a81e" "answer-c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" "answer-533a4289-18f4-5f2c-abf2-84425ff4dc32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f11f15bf-525a-5a41-bdc7-38a742a7f4d9" :answer/id #uuid "f11f15bf-525a-5a41-bdc7-38a742a7f4d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eb89bec-79dd-58af-8196-d3d356437ba3" :answer/id #uuid "3eb89bec-79dd-58af-8196-d3d356437ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\sqrt{y}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19341623-c069-57f0-918f-2be57be5a81e" :answer/id #uuid "19341623-c069-57f0-918f-2be57be5a81e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" :answer/id #uuid "c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533a4289-18f4-5f2c-abf2-84425ff4dc32" :answer/id #uuid "533a4289-18f4-5f2c-abf2-84425ff4dc32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-674cd7aa-b875-5b4c-b5f8-f279a4fd931b" :question/id #uuid "674cd7aa-b875-5b4c-b5f8-f279a4fd931b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143892" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2y\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =2({y}^{2})\\cdot 2|y| \\\\  & =4{y}^{2}|y|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9aa1eb7e-f2a5-549e-b007-219daa3b78ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9aa1eb7e-f2a5-549e-b007-219daa3b78ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa1eb7e-f2a5-549e-b007-219daa3b78ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bebaa45c-6d1d-5ae9-aa82-d69d094bf6a2" "answer-e32bb70d-69d8-5b13-87d0-5ff4152c7216" "answer-fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" "answer-83866d01-d00c-5606-97fc-8034676dfc07" "answer-af72d3b7-3cf4-586f-afbf-29d7d4a06a3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bebaa45c-6d1d-5ae9-aa82-d69d094bf6a2" :answer/id #uuid "bebaa45c-6d1d-5ae9-aa82-d69d094bf6a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2y, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e32bb70d-69d8-5b13-87d0-5ff4152c7216" :answer/id #uuid "e32bb70d-69d8-5b13-87d0-5ff4152c7216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{y}^{2}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" :answer/id #uuid "fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83866d01-d00c-5606-97fc-8034676dfc07" :answer/id #uuid "83866d01-d00c-5606-97fc-8034676dfc07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af72d3b7-3cf4-586f-afbf-29d7d4a06a3f" :answer/id #uuid "af72d3b7-3cf4-586f-afbf-29d7d4a06a3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162406 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{y})}{(\\frac{1}{y})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=1,\\,u(e)={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159638 :answer-field/correct "answer-643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" "answer-246d1671-2920-5107-938a-d5818924161b" "answer-408fabb1-72b8-5f80-b166-4b2097274a6b" "answer-94dcdc7d-dc32-5d1c-b053-4b6089835648" "answer-858132b6-0402-5b5e-b535-8e91c02ecb1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/id #uuid "643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246d1671-2920-5107-938a-d5818924161b" :answer/id #uuid "246d1671-2920-5107-938a-d5818924161b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{2}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-408fabb1-72b8-5f80-b166-4b2097274a6b" :answer/id #uuid "408fabb1-72b8-5f80-b166-4b2097274a6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94dcdc7d-dc32-5d1c-b053-4b6089835648" :answer/id #uuid "94dcdc7d-dc32-5d1c-b053-4b6089835648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{e-1}, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-858132b6-0402-5b5e-b535-8e91c02ecb1f" :answer/id #uuid "858132b6-0402-5b5e-b535-8e91c02ecb1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{y-2}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162279 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{y})\\cdot |{e}^{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{{e}^{y}}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y/2}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,u(4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158876 :answer-field/correct "answer-d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19b6e1b5-4780-54aa-8a89-2c389618ccea" "answer-d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" "answer-60d2bd5c-2906-5c77-9a51-8fcb2a54de03" "answer-9979ab92-aa46-551c-9b7a-d8c01b8826ab" "answer-aaaffa65-e7fc-5b99-b05e-ff602e8d8690"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19b6e1b5-4780-54aa-8a89-2c389618ccea" :answer/id #uuid "19b6e1b5-4780-54aa-8a89-2c389618ccea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}, & 1<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" :answer/id #uuid "d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60d2bd5c-2906-5c77-9a51-8fcb2a54de03" :answer/id #uuid "60d2bd5c-2906-5c77-9a51-8fcb2a54de03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y}, & e<y<{e}^{4} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9979ab92-aa46-551c-9b7a-d8c01b8826ab" :answer/id #uuid "9979ab92-aa46-551c-9b7a-d8c01b8826ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaaffa65-e7fc-5b99-b05e-ff602e8d8690" :answer/id #uuid "aaaffa65-e7fc-5b99-b05e-ff602e8d8690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 1<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163264 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable whose probability density function is known. We know that if $Y = u(X),$ then we can apply the distribution function method to compute the PDF of $Y.$\n\nIt's possible to generalize the distribution function method when $u(X)$ is strictly monotonic. This generalization is known as the **change-of-variables method**, and is stated below:\n\n*If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n![](images/0c/0cef4bed9fd4d4e598259a8082f29e9c20d65537c8eb30591c153870cd202724.svg)\n\nProof of this result will be provided at the end of the lesson. \n\nThe advantage of the change-of-variables method is that it's often easier and faster to implement than the distribution function method. The disadvantage is that it only works when $u$ is monotonic, whereas the distribution function method can be extended to deal with non-monotonic functions. \n\nFor example, suppose that $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/a3/a34a43fe641955493bc4e807d3dbd62bf47136a6e70278f9f7251a391da9bf80.svg)\n\nLet the random variable $Y = 3X.$ Then $u(x) = 3x,$ which is strictly monotonically increasing. Therefore, the change-of-variables method can be used to compute the PDF of $Y.$\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\nu^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad (u^{-1})'(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero $f_Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\qquad u(2) = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n![](images/4b/4bbe9255e26b47ee3d5afcb826dcc4c0da24e4786eb0ea74b964c2237829f0ae.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163272 :tutorial/content "Let's restate the change-of-variables method:\n\n*If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n![](images/3d/3dfea9417dbbdf6f9f4e828a29bef40d59b4d467fb08fee100d326a6bfd8e00b.svg)\n\nNow, let's prove this result. \n\nThe first part is simple. Because $X$ is between $a$ and $b,$ and $u$ is monotonic, $Y=u(X)$ must be between $u(a)$ and $u(b).$ So $f_Y(y) = 0$ when $y$ is not between $u(a)$ and $u(b).$\n\nTo carry out the rest of the proof, we will need to apply the distribution function method in two cases: \n\n- **Case 1**: $u$ is monotonically increasing\n\n- **Case 2**: $u$ is monotonically decreasing\n\nWe'll consider the case where $u$ is monotonically increasing only. The proof when $u$ is monotonically decreasing is similar.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative distribution of $Y.$ Assuming $y$ is between $u(a)$ and $u(b),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(u(X)\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{{u}^{-1}(y)}{f}_{X}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(t){|}_{a}^{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\  & ={F}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))-{F}_{X}(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we differentiate to get the probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[{F}_{Y}(y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[{F}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))-{F}_{X}(a)] \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $u$ is monotonically increasing, we have that $u^{-1}$ is monotonically increasing as well. To see this, it's helpful to remember that we reflect a function over the line $y=x$ to get its inverse.\n\nSince the slope of a monotonically increasing function is always zero or positive, we have $(u^{-1})'(y) \\geq 0,$ and consequently $(u^{-1})'(y) = \\left| (u^{-1})'(y) \\right|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))|({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162192 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838" :question/id #uuid "b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838" :question/math-academy-id "q-100121" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2(-1)+2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(2(1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(1-1)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-980312ed-2719-5060-9fbb-108e85ad677e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-980312ed-2719-5060-9fbb-108e85ad677e" :answer-field/id #uuid "980312ed-2719-5060-9fbb-108e85ad677e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63ad22ea-66fa-50fc-bde7-f0f232d88853" "answer-20c9cb4a-c332-5bc5-8dd5-fed7de433c7d" "answer-b938e797-07a9-546d-9bec-948b3e7a15fa" "answer-105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" "answer-68ff13dc-9101-5cf2-bc69-4e700b97b26f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63ad22ea-66fa-50fc-bde7-f0f232d88853" :answer/id #uuid "63ad22ea-66fa-50fc-bde7-f0f232d88853" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20c9cb4a-c332-5bc5-8dd5-fed7de433c7d" :answer/id #uuid "20c9cb4a-c332-5bc5-8dd5-fed7de433c7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b938e797-07a9-546d-9bec-948b3e7a15fa" :answer/id #uuid "b938e797-07a9-546d-9bec-948b3e7a15fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" :answer/id #uuid "105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ff13dc-9101-5cf2-bc69-4e700b97b26f" :answer/id #uuid "68ff13dc-9101-5cf2-bc69-4e700b97b26f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135104 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c63eea8e-a310-590a-8e20-2e3176065df1" :activity/id #uuid "c63eea8e-a310-590a-8e20-2e3176065df1" :activity/title "Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050508 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-d91d19e5-a7df-5edd-8015-8f17f5f765a7" "step-de8c8ba1-f427-5fd2-a778-745517d2b6d5" "step-e269519a-5f2e-5196-bc58-09cf2bbc2906"] :activity/first-step "step-d91d19e5-a7df-5edd-8015-8f17f5f765a7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d91d19e5-a7df-5edd-8015-8f17f5f765a7" :step/id #uuid "d91d19e5-a7df-5edd-8015-8f17f5f765a7" :step/content 17592186172378 :step/next "step-de8c8ba1-f427-5fd2-a778-745517d2b6d5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de8c8ba1-f427-5fd2-a778-745517d2b6d5" :step/id #uuid "de8c8ba1-f427-5fd2-a778-745517d2b6d5" :step/content 17592186278394 :step/next "step-e269519a-5f2e-5196-bc58-09cf2bbc2906" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e269519a-5f2e-5196-bc58-09cf2bbc2906" :step/id #uuid "e269519a-5f2e-5196-bc58-09cf2bbc2906" :step/content 17592186414463 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186159836 :answer-field/choices 17592186159840] [:db/retract 17592186159836 :answer-field/choices 17592186159841] [:db/retract 17592186159836 :answer-field/choices 17592186159837] [:db/retract 17592186159836 :answer-field/choices 17592186159838] [:db/retract 17592186159836 :answer-field/choices 17592186159839] {:db/id 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" "question-490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" "question-94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" :question/id #uuid "59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146398" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=72%=0.72,\\,n=80.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(80,0.72).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-282e375f-1850-5399-9599-cb2c5ef898b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-282e375f-1850-5399-9599-cb2c5ef898b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "282e375f-1850-5399-9599-cb2c5ef898b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a32f7fa-6742-56e1-bf1a-cf1c58e74768" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac7ce6d1-961d-56fe-aded-0106ed9f05c3" "answer-d72a2b1b-9ea0-537e-a5f6-1f5385cf75a5" "answer-9a32f7fa-6742-56e1-bf1a-cf1c58e74768" "answer-9e5f5792-ce54-5a1b-9b50-cfc7b9f8870e" "answer-22684d1d-b0b3-54ef-b3ee-48b2d6c88f60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac7ce6d1-961d-56fe-aded-0106ed9f05c3" :answer/id #uuid "ac7ce6d1-961d-56fe-aded-0106ed9f05c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(58,0.72)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d72a2b1b-9ea0-537e-a5f6-1f5385cf75a5" :answer/id #uuid "d72a2b1b-9ea0-537e-a5f6-1f5385cf75a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(80,0.28)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a32f7fa-6742-56e1-bf1a-cf1c58e74768" :answer/id #uuid "9a32f7fa-6742-56e1-bf1a-cf1c58e74768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(80,0.72)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e5f5792-ce54-5a1b-9b50-cfc7b9f8870e" :answer/id #uuid "9e5f5792-ce54-5a1b-9b50-cfc7b9f8870e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.28)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22684d1d-b0b3-54ef-b3ee-48b2d6c88f60" :answer/id #uuid "22684d1d-b0b3-54ef-b3ee-48b2d6c88f60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.72)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" :question/id #uuid "490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146401" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=200.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(200,0.6).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c2d22b3-82ab-571f-9d82-01f972b998da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c2d22b3-82ab-571f-9d82-01f972b998da" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d22b3-82ab-571f-9d82-01f972b998da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a13d411-f193-59df-a60d-691d14b62d42" :answer-field/choices ["answer-760d68c2-bf69-5092-a28f-9f206e7da7bb" "answer-a7ac58e6-6890-59aa-86e5-021020ca205b" "answer-8a13d411-f193-59df-a60d-691d14b62d42" "answer-7678dea6-b13b-5668-b51b-7fa42acc8ac1" "answer-c1b1149b-27d1-5022-b30e-bdf734dc8801"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-760d68c2-bf69-5092-a28f-9f206e7da7bb" :answer/id #uuid "760d68c2-bf69-5092-a28f-9f206e7da7bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(120,0.6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7ac58e6-6890-59aa-86e5-021020ca205b" :answer/id #uuid "a7ac58e6-6890-59aa-86e5-021020ca205b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(200,0.4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a13d411-f193-59df-a60d-691d14b62d42" :answer/id #uuid "8a13d411-f193-59df-a60d-691d14b62d42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(200,0.6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7678dea6-b13b-5668-b51b-7fa42acc8ac1" :answer/id #uuid "7678dea6-b13b-5668-b51b-7fa42acc8ac1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1b1149b-27d1-5022-b30e-bdf734dc8801" :answer/id #uuid "c1b1149b-27d1-5022-b30e-bdf734dc8801" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c" :question/id #uuid "94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-146405" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=5%=0.05,\\,n=120.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(120,0.05)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05\\cdot (1-0.05) \\\\  & =5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-620b9567-454a-5a74-9979-8ed7be2affd8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-620b9567-454a-5a74-9979-8ed7be2affd8" :answer-field/id #uuid "620b9567-454a-5a74-9979-8ed7be2affd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-669f46f9-7fe9-57c8-b15c-822e2852fd28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f7af2aa-2e28-5c1f-abd9-a7f96818713e" "answer-669f46f9-7fe9-57c8-b15c-822e2852fd28" "answer-6986251f-e45f-59ee-9667-7b37f30a8e1c" "answer-a0857c8a-f9dd-50e3-a78d-bf44e5e33721" "answer-1524fb62-acc2-536e-a99c-e6d653124ea2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f7af2aa-2e28-5c1f-abd9-a7f96818713e" :answer/id #uuid "7f7af2aa-2e28-5c1f-abd9-a7f96818713e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=6,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=5.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-669f46f9-7fe9-57c8-b15c-822e2852fd28" :answer/id #uuid "669f46f9-7fe9-57c8-b15c-822e2852fd28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=6,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=5.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6986251f-e45f-59ee-9667-7b37f30a8e1c" :answer/id #uuid "6986251f-e45f-59ee-9667-7b37f30a8e1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=6,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=6.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0857c8a-f9dd-50e3-a78d-bf44e5e33721" :answer/id #uuid "a0857c8a-f9dd-50e3-a78d-bf44e5e33721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=8,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=5.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1524fb62-acc2-536e-a99c-e6d653124ea2" :answer/id #uuid "1524fb62-acc2-536e-a99c-e6d653124ea2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=8,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=5.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" :question/id #uuid "a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-146445" :question/problem "Suppose that $38%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=38%=0.38,$ and $n=30.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =38%=0.38, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{30} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9340764-7d20-536c-8af6-aa8f9dc6fc8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9340764-7d20-536c-8af6-aa8f9dc6fc8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9340764-7d20-536c-8af6-aa8f9dc6fc8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68be19d6-216b-577d-b2f8-7073e6ae0ba3" "answer-bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" "answer-164226e5-15d4-5658-89e3-bcd50cd4659c" "answer-7fa95346-6c40-5885-9b17-5047d823cbf8" "answer-9b61f479-1547-598f-8162-e8595d51c610"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68be19d6-216b-577d-b2f8-7073e6ae0ba3" :answer/id #uuid "68be19d6-216b-577d-b2f8-7073e6ae0ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.006" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" :answer/id #uuid "bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.008" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-164226e5-15d4-5658-89e3-bcd50cd4659c" :answer/id #uuid "164226e5-15d4-5658-89e3-bcd50cd4659c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.011" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fa95346-6c40-5885-9b17-5047d823cbf8" :answer/id #uuid "7fa95346-6c40-5885-9b17-5047d823cbf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.62,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.008" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b61f479-1547-598f-8162-e8595d51c610" :answer/id #uuid "9b61f479-1547-598f-8162-e8595d51c610" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.62,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.011" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162439 :question/problem "It is known that $15%$ of a particular teenage population ride bicycles for exercise. A random sample of $n=60$ teenagers is taken from the population and asked whether they ride a bicycle for exercise.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of teenagers in the sample who ride bicycles? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=15%=0.15,$ and $n=60.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =15%=0.15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{60} \\\\  & ≈0.0021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159836 :answer-field/correct "answer-083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" "answer-2c027381-40ea-5799-9ee6-5c1914e36adb" "answer-e788456d-4b2f-5a3b-9773-97044c05216e" "answer-fa98449f-940e-591b-be82-68f3da8ba744" "answer-d70aa7b2-f393-5544-ab75-58709dc4953c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" :answer/id #uuid "083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0021" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c027381-40ea-5799-9ee6-5c1914e36adb" :answer/id #uuid "2c027381-40ea-5799-9ee6-5c1914e36adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0025" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e788456d-4b2f-5a3b-9773-97044c05216e" :answer/id #uuid "e788456d-4b2f-5a3b-9773-97044c05216e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0036" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa98449f-940e-591b-be82-68f3da8ba744" :answer/id #uuid "fa98449f-940e-591b-be82-68f3da8ba744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0021" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d70aa7b2-f393-5544-ab75-58709dc4953c" :answer/id #uuid "d70aa7b2-f393-5544-ab75-58709dc4953c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0036" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-566bafbe-17d8-59ff-ae1a-7f0098a7748d" :activity/id #uuid "566bafbe-17d8-59ff-ae1a-7f0098a7748d" :activity/title "Sampling Proportions From Finite Populations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048327 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-774983eb-fe83-513b-8f77-ab281c6af635" "step-3ded7ce8-7b5b-5a82-bc65-9550d172352f" "step-0eb51c85-c2ef-507e-b1a1-aca1befe52a0" "step-a6662e34-cc38-5218-b94a-784dde3dea35" "step-416e8b5c-3b97-5021-9133-e508e5a83961"] :activity/first-step "step-774983eb-fe83-513b-8f77-ab281c6af635" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-774983eb-fe83-513b-8f77-ab281c6af635" :step/id #uuid "774983eb-fe83-513b-8f77-ab281c6af635" :step/content "question-59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" :step/next "step-3ded7ce8-7b5b-5a82-bc65-9550d172352f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3ded7ce8-7b5b-5a82-bc65-9550d172352f" :step/id #uuid "3ded7ce8-7b5b-5a82-bc65-9550d172352f" :step/content "question-490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" :step/next "step-0eb51c85-c2ef-507e-b1a1-aca1befe52a0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0eb51c85-c2ef-507e-b1a1-aca1befe52a0" :step/id #uuid "0eb51c85-c2ef-507e-b1a1-aca1befe52a0" :step/content "question-94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c" :step/next "step-a6662e34-cc38-5218-b94a-784dde3dea35" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a6662e34-cc38-5218-b94a-784dde3dea35" :step/id #uuid "a6662e34-cc38-5218-b94a-784dde3dea35" :step/content "question-a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" :step/next "step-416e8b5c-3b97-5021-9133-e508e5a83961" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-416e8b5c-3b97-5021-9133-e508e5a83961" :step/id #uuid "416e8b5c-3b97-5021-9133-e508e5a83961" :step/content 17592186162439 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" "question-e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" "question-3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" :question/id #uuid "0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" :question/math-academy-id "q-159748" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5-2 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=7,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+7+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,14).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-70fb0548-9e58-5eec-98d2-d882b0569a5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-70fb0548-9e58-5eec-98d2-d882b0569a5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "70fb0548-9e58-5eec-98d2-d882b0569a5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-daee6f33-0dfe-59c6-8639-bc8f66d75e00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2846048f-2417-521c-9d91-127a15b07b36" "answer-daee6f33-0dfe-59c6-8639-bc8f66d75e00" "answer-2bd86d90-af5c-5230-b370-aa075ec9497c" "answer-370d24aa-0151-57e8-bd1a-dbbffce961f6" "answer-3d47ed6b-2a88-5d85-914f-29d9c5213390"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2846048f-2417-521c-9d91-127a15b07b36" :answer/id #uuid "2846048f-2417-521c-9d91-127a15b07b36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daee6f33-0dfe-59c6-8639-bc8f66d75e00" :answer/id #uuid "daee6f33-0dfe-59c6-8639-bc8f66d75e00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-7,14)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bd86d90-af5c-5230-b370-aa075ec9497c" :answer/id #uuid "2bd86d90-af5c-5230-b370-aa075ec9497c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-7,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-370d24aa-0151-57e8-bd1a-dbbffce961f6" :answer/id #uuid "370d24aa-0151-57e8-bd1a-dbbffce961f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(3,14)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d47ed6b-2a88-5d85-914f-29d9c5213390" :answer/id #uuid "3d47ed6b-2a88-5d85-914f-29d9c5213390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(3,\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" :question/id #uuid "e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" :question/math-academy-id "q-159749" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=3,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+12-24 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(11,33).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f442e1a-b907-5d38-960a-0590874e2f6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f442e1a-b907-5d38-960a-0590874e2f6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f442e1a-b907-5d38-960a-0590874e2f6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0cd36490-edd9-5214-a621-f23cfa7c2994" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cd36490-edd9-5214-a621-f23cfa7c2994" "answer-5962f9df-804d-53d5-9166-dfb18748621f" "answer-70d55769-d43e-5f7b-bd36-e8679f82da3f" "answer-cf1fdf93-08d1-5f0d-a2b7-865f4471e783" "answer-670e1e26-754f-56e5-843b-304f893b1022"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd36490-edd9-5214-a621-f23cfa7c2994" :answer/id #uuid "0cd36490-edd9-5214-a621-f23cfa7c2994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(11,33)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5962f9df-804d-53d5-9166-dfb18748621f" :answer/id #uuid "5962f9df-804d-53d5-9166-dfb18748621f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(11,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70d55769-d43e-5f7b-bd36-e8679f82da3f" :answer/id #uuid "70d55769-d43e-5f7b-bd36-e8679f82da3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(5,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf1fdf93-08d1-5f0d-a2b7-865f4471e783" :answer/id #uuid "cf1fdf93-08d1-5f0d-a2b7-865f4471e783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(5,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-670e1e26-754f-56e5-843b-304f893b1022" :answer/id #uuid "670e1e26-754f-56e5-843b-304f893b1022" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(7,33)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250" :question/id #uuid "3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250" :question/math-academy-id "q-159750" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}-4\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5+4 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot 3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =75+32-80 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-1,27).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-811b92f6-defd-55cc-8044-3d28a01fece4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-811b92f6-defd-55cc-8044-3d28a01fece4" :answer-field/id #uuid "811b92f6-defd-55cc-8044-3d28a01fece4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea29a78b-d5bd-58c5-bf5a-061d39d47053" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54c9603b-92f1-56f6-99ca-2927d578549b" "answer-ea29a78b-d5bd-58c5-bf5a-061d39d47053" "answer-fccad168-961f-5565-88d9-b5040f528958" "answer-6352137f-a35d-5422-a665-50d3360c88ee" "answer-a88422ce-eaac-5228-b582-ab5651637d03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54c9603b-92f1-56f6-99ca-2927d578549b" :answer/id #uuid "54c9603b-92f1-56f6-99ca-2927d578549b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,18)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea29a78b-d5bd-58c5-bf5a-061d39d47053" :answer/id #uuid "ea29a78b-d5bd-58c5-bf5a-061d39d47053" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,27)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fccad168-961f-5565-88d9-b5040f528958" :answer/id #uuid "fccad168-961f-5565-88d9-b5040f528958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6352137f-a35d-5422-a665-50d3360c88ee" :answer/id #uuid "6352137f-a35d-5422-a665-50d3360c88ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-9,18)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a88422ce-eaac-5228-b582-ab5651637d03" :answer/id #uuid "a88422ce-eaac-5228-b582-ab5651637d03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-9,27)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" "question-54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" :question/id #uuid "4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" :question/math-academy-id "q-159804" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y\\le -2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le -2).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le -2) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{-2-(-7)}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1) & =Φ(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1)=0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le -2)=0.8413.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e5cc2f0-6725-5001-8935-9b10d946cfa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e5cc2f0-6725-5001-8935-9b10d946cfa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5cc2f0-6725-5001-8935-9b10d946cfa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-52750003-2d76-57fb-a067-cb80127c33cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5189a220-76ea-5091-b49a-16f052b2f1d4" "answer-e4152bbf-4ef2-5a5d-a6e2-4af52f0157e1" "answer-52750003-2d76-57fb-a067-cb80127c33cc" "answer-82993aab-8bd6-5d61-9861-50ee8d1879b5" "answer-37c00ba7-4e07-54ad-bdbc-1156749cc2e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5189a220-76ea-5091-b49a-16f052b2f1d4" :answer/id #uuid "5189a220-76ea-5091-b49a-16f052b2f1d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8138" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4152bbf-4ef2-5a5d-a6e2-4af52f0157e1" :answer/id #uuid "e4152bbf-4ef2-5a5d-a6e2-4af52f0157e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8359" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52750003-2d76-57fb-a067-cb80127c33cc" :answer/id #uuid "52750003-2d76-57fb-a067-cb80127c33cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8413" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82993aab-8bd6-5d61-9861-50ee8d1879b5" :answer/id #uuid "82993aab-8bd6-5d61-9861-50ee8d1879b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8587" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37c00ba7-4e07-54ad-bdbc-1156749cc2e3" :answer/id #uuid "37c00ba7-4e07-54ad-bdbc-1156749cc2e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8641" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e" :question/id #uuid "54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159922" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7673\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7704\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7995\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8023\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8238\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9564\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9573\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9641\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9649\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9656\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9713\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9719\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9726\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2+0+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+6+20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(0,40).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-6<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-6-0}{\\sqrt{40}}<Z\\le \\frac{12-0}{\\sqrt{40}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90) & =P(Z\\le 1.90)-P(Z\\le -0.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.90)-Φ(-0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(1.90) & =0.9713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.95)=1-Φ(0.95)=1-0.8289=0.1711.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)=0.9713-0.1711=0.8002.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-6<Y\\le 12)=0.8002.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5147a116-059b-5bc9-9e03-f4ccc1268a8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5147a116-059b-5bc9-9e03-f4ccc1268a8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5147a116-059b-5bc9-9e03-f4ccc1268a8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b48f9bd-b48b-5648-9060-b92aee8c6760" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b48f9bd-b48b-5648-9060-b92aee8c6760" "answer-11f333d9-733e-5389-8cda-5e1dfd790b4f" "answer-4c5cc44d-5d18-5731-a82e-007a6010f363" "answer-eb6bab9b-a8ad-52b9-8e04-2ce38c845042" "answer-2270f878-d33b-5b4b-94a5-97c69457888a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b48f9bd-b48b-5648-9060-b92aee8c6760" :answer/id #uuid "6b48f9bd-b48b-5648-9060-b92aee8c6760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8002" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11f333d9-733e-5389-8cda-5e1dfd790b4f" :answer/id #uuid "11f333d9-733e-5389-8cda-5e1dfd790b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8289" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c5cc44d-5d18-5731-a82e-007a6010f363" :answer/id #uuid "4c5cc44d-5d18-5731-a82e-007a6010f363" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8611" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb6bab9b-a8ad-52b9-8e04-2ce38c845042" :answer/id #uuid "eb6bab9b-a8ad-52b9-8e04-2ce38c845042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9424" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2270f878-d33b-5b4b-94a5-97c69457888a" :answer/id #uuid "2270f878-d33b-5b4b-94a5-97c69457888a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9713" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-efce7d92-bdf5-5126-8ee2-95a76a4e27f8" :activity/id #uuid "efce7d92-bdf5-5126-8ee2-95a76a4e27f8" :activity/title "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050612 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-d6927984-d02e-577e-8182-e0ef43721475" "step-01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" "step-d04d0fda-bcd1-5335-8424-d33cc219d276" "step-30857c75-58d2-5428-a216-a30804c4b0dd" "step-99861a14-37fb-5d5b-8d38-23e43e22fe20"] :activity/first-step "step-d6927984-d02e-577e-8182-e0ef43721475" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d6927984-d02e-577e-8182-e0ef43721475" :step/id #uuid "d6927984-d02e-577e-8182-e0ef43721475" :step/content "question-0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" :step/next "step-01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" :step/id #uuid "01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" :step/content "question-e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" :step/next "step-d04d0fda-bcd1-5335-8424-d33cc219d276" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d04d0fda-bcd1-5335-8424-d33cc219d276" :step/id #uuid "d04d0fda-bcd1-5335-8424-d33cc219d276" :step/content "question-3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250" :step/next "step-30857c75-58d2-5428-a216-a30804c4b0dd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-30857c75-58d2-5428-a216-a30804c4b0dd" :step/id #uuid "30857c75-58d2-5428-a216-a30804c4b0dd" :step/content "question-4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" :step/next "step-99861a14-37fb-5d5b-8d38-23e43e22fe20" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-99861a14-37fb-5d5b-8d38-23e43e22fe20" :step/id #uuid "99861a14-37fb-5d5b-8d38-23e43e22fe20" :step/content "question-54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" :question/id #uuid "763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-22902" :question/problem "A right triangle has legs of length $2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." :question/worked-solution "Let $a=2\\,cm,$ $b=4\\,cm,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f1768e5-8700-506e-98db-0bb9abb61815"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f1768e5-8700-506e-98db-0bb9abb61815" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f1768e5-8700-506e-98db-0bb9abb61815" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" :answer-field/choices ["answer-636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" "answer-0664bfa8-2d01-5d3d-ad93-e8fca7955080" "answer-31089567-fd42-54a4-a8d0-48cfa5c4e330" "answer-7d8e0d22-8c98-5dcf-9e27-c996945628ff" "answer-e9a534c8-2d50-5c60-9824-ae02704c4eca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" :answer/id #uuid "636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0664bfa8-2d01-5d3d-ad93-e8fca7955080" :answer/id #uuid "0664bfa8-2d01-5d3d-ad93-e8fca7955080" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31089567-fd42-54a4-a8d0-48cfa5c4e330" :answer/id #uuid "31089567-fd42-54a4-a8d0-48cfa5c4e330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d8e0d22-8c98-5dcf-9e27-c996945628ff" :answer/id #uuid "7d8e0d22-8c98-5dcf-9e27-c996945628ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9a534c8-2d50-5c60-9824-ae02704c4eca" :answer/id #uuid "e9a534c8-2d50-5c60-9824-ae02704c4eca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" :question/id #uuid "29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" :question/math-academy-id "q-67769" :question/problem "![](images/16/16e8f82922ebd5d4b10729e2935c893dd59a7dfb8faa69dde4567c7036209d7d.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $AC=BC$ and $\\overset{⟷}{CD}⊥\\overset{―}{AB}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the point $C$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, $\\overset{⟷}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $DA=DB.$ We can use this equation so solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DA & =DB \\\\ 3x-3 & =x+1 \\\\ 3x-x & =1+3 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-436bedcf-5ab9-57ff-b69c-7f7c75355960"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-436bedcf-5ab9-57ff-b69c-7f7c75355960" :answer-field/id #uuid "436bedcf-5ab9-57ff-b69c-7f7c75355960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8cdeab7-dd91-5581-b022-bc74bf4ee7c3" "answer-1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" "answer-335ced4a-c213-5d68-a762-44d841c873a7" "answer-c92d8c81-6d47-5ef1-a7b6-44a2a484001c" "answer-5aa97ed3-0a0c-50fd-8572-26ade33ad99b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8cdeab7-dd91-5581-b022-bc74bf4ee7c3" :answer/id #uuid "c8cdeab7-dd91-5581-b022-bc74bf4ee7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" :answer/id #uuid "1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-335ced4a-c213-5d68-a762-44d841c873a7" :answer/id #uuid "335ced4a-c213-5d68-a762-44d841c873a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c92d8c81-6d47-5ef1-a7b6-44a2a484001c" :answer/id #uuid "c92d8c81-6d47-5ef1-a7b6-44a2a484001c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aa97ed3-0a0c-50fd-8572-26ade33ad99b" :answer/id #uuid "5aa97ed3-0a0c-50fd-8572-26ade33ad99b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073887 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57" :question/id #uuid "ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-60049" :question/problem "Isabella took $2$ hours to wash $12$ windows. If she washes the windows at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" :question/worked-solution "The ratio of washed windows to hours is $12:2.$ So we start with the ratio $12:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27412f37-44b6-5f69-9944-94e2934ed7dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27412f37-44b6-5f69-9944-94e2934ed7dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "27412f37-44b6-5f69-9944-94e2934ed7dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbde90ad-dcd7-5a36-ad24-53913d79dc77" "answer-0777c429-4126-5227-8671-6a34e94d7cb6" "answer-bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" "answer-58842088-1320-504a-b699-b86637aaf14a" "answer-c896cb4d-9d0d-516c-ac40-a9ffe1766b52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbde90ad-dcd7-5a36-ad24-53913d79dc77" :answer/id #uuid "cbde90ad-dcd7-5a36-ad24-53913d79dc77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Isabella washed $2$ windows per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0777c429-4126-5227-8671-6a34e94d7cb6" :answer/id #uuid "0777c429-4126-5227-8671-6a34e94d7cb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Isabella washed $3$ windows per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" :answer/id #uuid "bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58842088-1320-504a-b699-b86637aaf14a" :answer/id #uuid "58842088-1320-504a-b699-b86637aaf14a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Isabella washed $\\frac{1}{3}$ of a window per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c896cb4d-9d0d-516c-ac40-a9ffe1766b52" :answer/id #uuid "c896cb4d-9d0d-516c-ac40-a9ffe1766b52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Isabella washed $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a window per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" :question/id #uuid "d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-54292" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db217f00-30c9-50d9-a7c1-29d4b1d834be" "answer-347c2f0c-5a52-5eed-a5f6-845a1b198245" "answer-95cbbbb8-182c-54d0-b493-2f62f281a0dc" "answer-dbc989c2-ca66-5d8e-9dc2-695894977ce3" "answer-48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db217f00-30c9-50d9-a7c1-29d4b1d834be" :answer/id #uuid "db217f00-30c9-50d9-a7c1-29d4b1d834be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-347c2f0c-5a52-5eed-a5f6-845a1b198245" :answer/id #uuid "347c2f0c-5a52-5eed-a5f6-845a1b198245" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95cbbbb8-182c-54d0-b493-2f62f281a0dc" :answer/id #uuid "95cbbbb8-182c-54d0-b493-2f62f281a0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbc989c2-ca66-5d8e-9dc2-695894977ce3" :answer/id #uuid "dbc989c2-ca66-5d8e-9dc2-695894977ce3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/id #uuid "48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" :question/id #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-110680" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer-field/choices ["answer-738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" "answer-39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" "answer-e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" "answer-a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" "answer-4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" :answer/id #uuid "738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}z}{y}+3x{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" :answer/id #uuid "e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-4{x}^{4}z+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" :answer/id #uuid "a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z}{y}+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6" :answer/id #uuid "4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}z}{y}+6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3" :question/id #uuid "5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76803" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff7f82365a0b2253c43ad441a4af93354ae54ae027fbd2390634b64728cfb210.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠AOB.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $∠COF$ is a straight angle. So, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD+m∠DOE+m∠EOF & =m∠COF \\\\ {75}^{∘}+m∠DOE+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠DOE & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $∠AOB$ and $∠DOE$ are vertical angles. Therefore, since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOB=m∠DOE={30}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a065792-7ae1-5541-a4f8-b06c6ad6b15b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a065792-7ae1-5541-a4f8-b06c6ad6b15b" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a065792-7ae1-5541-a4f8-b06c6ad6b15b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-debc1515-abd7-599d-ac45-08a3bd014040" "answer-6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" "answer-eeccccad-b6d9-5394-b737-bfc9043dc537" "answer-85fae8db-017c-5ed0-a8a5-edacd74b5ee5" "answer-8ae0ff7c-743d-565a-adf6-91feb5251b55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-debc1515-abd7-599d-ac45-08a3bd014040" :answer/id #uuid "debc1515-abd7-599d-ac45-08a3bd014040" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" :answer/id #uuid "6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeccccad-b6d9-5394-b737-bfc9043dc537" :answer/id #uuid "eeccccad-b6d9-5394-b737-bfc9043dc537" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85fae8db-017c-5ed0-a8a5-edacd74b5ee5" :answer/id #uuid "85fae8db-017c-5ed0-a8a5-edacd74b5ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ae0ff7c-743d-565a-adf6-91feb5251b55" :answer/id #uuid "8ae0ff7c-743d-565a-adf6-91feb5251b55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04" :question/id #uuid "1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04" :question/math-academy-id "q-86553" :question/problem "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?" :question/worked-solution "The card's value is $$50$, and this number increases by $8%$ each year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0db388ed-bde6-5229-8b58-1ef5e13f677d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0db388ed-bde6-5229-8b58-1ef5e13f677d" :answer-field/id #uuid "0db388ed-bde6-5229-8b58-1ef5e13f677d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c333b68a-f76f-541c-8467-a1c446d09e3a" "answer-34c59582-bd38-5896-aa78-0791b61291ad" "answer-22157acf-db7c-512b-8c26-ddd6528d48c2" "answer-be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" "answer-4a5f0374-d9de-5c31-9172-a64c1ef6174d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c333b68a-f76f-541c-8467-a1c446d09e3a" :answer/id #uuid "c333b68a-f76f-541c-8467-a1c446d09e3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\cdot (0.08)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34c59582-bd38-5896-aa78-0791b61291ad" :answer/id #uuid "34c59582-bd38-5896-aa78-0791b61291ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\cdot (0.8)^{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22157acf-db7c-512b-8c26-ddd6528d48c2" :answer/id #uuid "22157acf-db7c-512b-8c26-ddd6528d48c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\cdot (1.8)^{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" :answer/id #uuid "be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a5f0374-d9de-5c31-9172-a64c1ef6174d" :answer/id #uuid "4a5f0374-d9de-5c31-9172-a64c1ef6174d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\cdot (1.8)^{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" :question/id #uuid "06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" :question/math-academy-id "q-288569" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer/id #uuid "b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192634 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3" :question/id #uuid "72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-212479" :question/problem "Consider the absolute value equation\n\n$$\n3|x+1|-4=8.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|x+1|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we add $4$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1|-4 & =8 \\\\ 3|x+1|-4+4 & =8+4 \\\\ 3|x+1| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1| & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3|x+1|}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}|x+1|}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ |x+1| & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06e19274-3bee-5fd0-8c5c-ee3a6c3f2176"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06e19274-3bee-5fd0-8c5c-ee3a6c3f2176" :answer-field/id #uuid "06e19274-3bee-5fd0-8c5c-ee3a6c3f2176" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0" :answer/id #uuid "77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105706 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531" :question/id #uuid "97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531" :question/math-academy-id "q-212910" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot (\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00b9f5df-6016-5b3f-a644-4963b4ea3a42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00b9f5df-6016-5b3f-a644-4963b4ea3a42" :answer-field/id #uuid "00b9f5df-6016-5b3f-a644-4963b4ea3a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5573251c-6bf9-557c-a86c-d0de8177c93e" "answer-7e6d0021-df51-5890-84d9-4c364481d3fd" "answer-fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" "answer-813abaaf-ebbb-5bad-8723-5b8696d33fab" "answer-4a214e20-11ee-5746-8810-076a062c13ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5573251c-6bf9-557c-a86c-d0de8177c93e" :answer/id #uuid "5573251c-6bf9-557c-a86c-d0de8177c93e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e6d0021-df51-5890-84d9-4c364481d3fd" :answer/id #uuid "7e6d0021-df51-5890-84d9-4c364481d3fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" :answer/id #uuid "fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-813abaaf-ebbb-5bad-8723-5b8696d33fab" :answer/id #uuid "813abaaf-ebbb-5bad-8723-5b8696d33fab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a214e20-11ee-5746-8810-076a062c13ec" :answer/id #uuid "4a214e20-11ee-5746-8810-076a062c13ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a07beb96-a59e-501d-8f97-4e4432ece9fc" :activity/id #uuid "a07beb96-a59e-501d-8f97-4e4432ece9fc" :activity/title "Quiz 17" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-3ef7c913-7744-593d-a71d-b97eb6eccdee" "step-49b487d5-41bd-53aa-a5f9-930e21fe528b" "step-b95bbee2-698a-5b3a-8d3b-02cfcf7dc416" "step-e5ef0965-017c-5a18-928c-97b772289a49" "step-7def1e24-b0f7-53d6-b2ff-05c3d98e74ea" "step-5d4d47af-47b9-5f7a-95fe-8cab364dcf4c" "step-577ca3a5-901e-518d-a8a8-67bc623b6661" "step-3c2007c4-dec8-59c7-b6b3-8d9aa6d6d7d9" "step-e5d6775b-d9f1-5c36-987c-4c5b6c60ddbf" "step-674d8d23-b9aa-5600-a167-f706632680da"] :activity/first-step "step-3ef7c913-7744-593d-a71d-b97eb6eccdee" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3ef7c913-7744-593d-a71d-b97eb6eccdee" :step/id #uuid "3ef7c913-7744-593d-a71d-b97eb6eccdee" :step/content "question-763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" :step/next "step-49b487d5-41bd-53aa-a5f9-930e21fe528b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-49b487d5-41bd-53aa-a5f9-930e21fe528b" :step/id #uuid "49b487d5-41bd-53aa-a5f9-930e21fe528b" :step/content "question-29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" :step/next "step-b95b